  { id:'opqb-t14-582', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Primary optic atrophy** results from:',
    options:['Retinal disease','Chronic glaucoma','Papilledema','Neurological disease'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Primary optic atrophy\n\n▪ Damage of the optic nerve occurring outside of the eyeball\n\n▪ Causes:\n\n1. Congenital\n2. Demyelinating disease\n3. Syphilis\n4. Compression by intracranial or orbital tumours\n\n**Secondary optic atrophy**\n\n▪ Due to long-standing papilledema or papillitis. (2ry → 2 causes)\n\n**Consecutive optic atrophy**\n\n▪ Which occurs due to retinal diseases e.g., Retinitis pigmentosa\n▪ Retinal examination shows the primary cause of the optic atrophy\n\n**Glaucomatous optic atrophy**\n\n▪ Caused by end-stage chronic glaucoma\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.100. It is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194's four-type table reproduced almost word for word, and it answers the question by elimination: each of the three distractors is the cause of a DIFFERENT type.)*\n\n| Option | The type it actually causes |\n|---|---|\n| **A** Retinal disease | **Consecutive** — \"Retinal examination shows the primary cause\" |\n| **B** Chronic glaucoma | **Glaucomatous** — end-stage chronic glaucoma |\n| **C** Papilledema | **Secondary** — with papillitis, the box's \"2 causes\" |\n| **D** Neurological disease | **Primary** — demyelinating disease, syphilis, intracranial compression |\n\n**What the fundus shows, from the lecture.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14 prints the four types as four captions on four photographs and defines none of them, so the pictures carry the difference: **Primary** is a *chalky white* disc with **clearly defined, sharp margins**, normal-calibre vessels and a normal red-orange retina; **Secondary** is a *dirty grey-white* disc with **indistinct, blurred margins** fading into a fuzzy halo — the residue of the swelling that preceded it; **Consecutive** is a *waxy yellow* disc with **markedly attenuated arterioles** on a washed-out retina; **Glaucomatous** confines the pallor **to the cup**, with a sharp disc margin.\n\nThat margin is the whole distinction between A's answer and C's. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 states it in Tutorial-marked text on both sides of the table: primary \"occurs without previous swelling of the optic nerve head\", secondary \"is preceded by swelling of the optic nerve head\". A nerve that dies where you cannot see it — behind the globe, in the canal, at the chiasm — atrophies without ever having swollen ophthalmoscopically, and its edge stays clean.\n\n**Why \"neurological disease\" is the right label for that group.** All four printed causes of primary atrophy sit behind or outside the eye and are neurological rather than ocular: congenital, demyelinating (multiple sclerosis, the retrobulbar form of optic neuritis), syphilis, and compression by an intracranial or orbital tumour. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 also notes the one fact common to all four types — \"The optic nerve appears pale in all types of optic atrophy due to decreased vascularity\" — and that **there is no treatment for optic atrophy** of any type.",
    objective:'Primary optic atrophy is the pallor that follows damage to the optic nerve behind the eyeball — congenital, demyelinating, syphilitic or compressive — and it has sharp disc margins because no antecedent disc swelling occurred.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q582), key + explanation p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-583', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Retro-bulbar optic neuritis** is characterized by:',
    options:['Marked swelling of the optic disc.','Impaired direct light reflex in the affected eye','Impaired consensual light reflex in the affected eye','Normal visual acuity'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Retrobulbar means behind the globe, so the disc looks normal and only the pupil and the vision betray the nerve.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 classifies optic neuritis into three forms and tags the second one in the classification box itself: \"2. Retrobulbar neuritis **[normal optic disc]**\". Its signs list opens with the same statement — \"The optic disc may appear completely normal\" — followed by \"Afferent pupillary defect · Decreased visual acuity · Impaired colour vision · Visual field defects\".\n\n**Why B.** The afferent limb of the light reflex is the diseased optic nerve. Light shone into the affected eye is carried badly, so **both** pupils constrict poorly — but the deficit is *elicited* as an impaired **direct** response in that eye, which is what the option names. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 6 photographs exactly this as the *Marcus Gunn Pupil*: four frames of a swinging-flashlight test in which the torch on the good eye constricts both pupils and the torch on the bad eye lets both dilate again.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — marked swelling of the optic disc.** That is the *papillitis* form of optic neuritis, the one in which the inflammation sits at the nerve head. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.189 defines papillitis as \"Inflammatory edema of the disc\". A swollen disc rules retrobulbar out by definition.\n- **C — impaired consensual reflex in the affected eye.** The consensual response *of* the affected eye is driven by light entering the **fellow, healthy** eye, and its efferent limb is that eye's third nerve, which is untouched. So the affected pupil still constricts briskly when the good eye is lit. Options B and C are a deliberate pair, and the trap is forgetting which eye is being illuminated.\n- **D — normal visual acuity.** Vision falls, and that is the presenting complaint. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191 lists \"Subacute monocular visual impairment\" and \"Decreased visual acuity\"; `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8's handwritten table gives papillitis a down-arrow for VA against a plain \"N\" for papilledema.\n\n**The examiner's summary of the paradox:** in retrobulbar neuritis *the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing* — the classical formulation of a normal disc with a severely reduced acuity. It is not printed in either deck or in the book, and is offered here as an aide-memoire only, **not taken from the course material**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retrobulbar neuritis leaves the disc looking normal and declares itself through a relative afferent pupillary defect — an impaired direct light response in the affected eye — with reduced acuity and colour vision.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q583), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-584', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'The type of **optic atrophy that follows retro-bulbar neuritis** is:',
    options:['Secondary optic atrophy','Consecutive optic atrophy','Glaucomatous optic atrophy','Primary optic atrophy'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The bank keys A, secondary optic atrophy. The course material, read on its own terms, points at D.** Both readings are set out here; the key printed by the bank is the one recorded above and is not disputed.\n\n**What the book prints.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 tabulates the four types:\n\n| Type | The book's definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Primary** | \"damage of the optic nerve occurring outside of the eyeball\"; causes — congenital, **demyelinating disease (MS): recurrent attacks**, syphilis, compression by intracranial or orbital tumours; \"occurs **without** previous swelling of the optic nerve head\" |\n| **Secondary** | \"Due to long-standing **papilledema or papillitis**\"; \"It is **preceded by swelling** of the optic nerve head\" |\n| **Consecutive** | \"occurs due to retinal diseases e.g., Retinitis pigmentosa\" |\n| **Glaucomatous** | \"Caused by end stage chronic glaucoma\" |\n\nThe discriminating fact is antecedent disc swelling. Retrobulbar neuritis is defined by the *absence* of disc swelling: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 tags it \"[normal optic disc]\", and Q643 of this very topic keys \"May appear completely normal\" for the disc in retrobulbar neuritis. The book also files **demyelinating disease** — the commonest cause of retrobulbar neuritis (Q603 keys it) — under **primary**. On the book's own definitions the atrophy that follows retrobulbar neuritis is therefore primary.\n\n**How the keyed answer can be defended.** The book's secondary column names \"papillitis\", and papillitis and retrobulbar neuritis are two forms of one disease, optic neuritis, so a reader who treats \"post-neuritic atrophy\" as a single category will land on secondary. Some texts also use *secondary* to mean any atrophy consequent on inflammation, reserving *primary* for atrophy with no identified antecedent. That usage is **not taken from the course material**; neither deck defines the four types at all — `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14 prints them as four captions on four photographs and its own register records \"THE FOUR TYPES OF OPTIC ATROPHY ARE NEVER DEFINED\".\n\n**Why B and C are wrong on any reading.** **Consecutive** requires a primary *retinal* disease, and its fundus signs are the giveaway — a waxy pale disc with grossly attenuated vessels on an abnormal retina (`L21` slides 14 and 16, the second showing coarse peripheral bone-spicule pigment). **Glaucomatous** requires end-stage chronic glaucoma and confines the pallor to a deeply excavated cup with a sharp disc margin (`L21` slides 10–14). Neither has any relation to an inflamed optic nerve.\n\n**How to use this entry.** Learn the book's rule — *previous swelling or not* — because it decides primary against secondary in every other question. Learn the bank's key for this question, because it is what the paper marks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The four types of optic atrophy are separated by whether the disc swelled first; retrobulbar neuritis leaves a normal disc, which is why this key is worth reading against the book\'s own definition rather than memorised alone.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q584), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-585', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'A male patient **30 years old** with visual acuity of 6/6 in both eyes. **Twelve hours ago**, he presented himself with a drop of vision of the left eye. On examination, visual acuity was **6/6 in the right eye and 6/60 in the left eye**. Fundus examination showed **blurred edges of the left optic disc**. The most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Raised intra cranial pressure','Raised ocular tension','Central Retinal Artery Occlusion','Optic neuritis'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Four findings, and each one excludes a different option: unilateral, acute, marked acuity loss, swollen disc.**\n\nThe swollen disc narrows the field to two diseases — papilledema and papillitis — and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.189 prints the table that separates them:\n\n| | Papilledema | Papillitis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Laterality** | Bilateral | **Unilateral** |\n| **Symptoms** | VA usually normal; transient blurring; headache and vomiting | **Marked visual deterioration** |\n| **Pupils** | Normal | RAPD |\n| **Colour vision** | Normal | Defective |\n| **Disc swelling** | More | Less |\n| **Disc hyperaemia** | Less | More |\n| **Field** | Enlarged blind spot | Central scotoma |\n\nThis patient is **unilateral** (right disc not mentioned as abnormal, right VA 6/6) and has **6/60 twelve hours in** — both rows point to papillitis, which is the disc-swelling form of **optic neuritis**. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8's handwritten table says the same in the lecturer's shorthand: Laterality \"Bilateral (Except Foster Kennedy)\" against \"Unilateral (Except NMO)\", VA \"N\" against \"↓\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — raised intracranial pressure.** This is papilledema, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 defines it as \"Swelling of the optic disc secondary to increased intracranial pressure, **It is typically bilateral**\", adding that \"Vision is not affected early in the condition but transient visual obscuration or transient attacks of b[l]urred vision may be present\". A one-sided disc with 6/60 at twelve hours is the opposite of that picture on both counts.\n- **B — raised ocular tension.** Acute angle-closure raises the pressure and drops the vision, but it is painful, red, with a hazy cornea and a fixed mid-dilated pupil, and the disc it eventually damages is **cupped**, not blurred. The stem records neither pain nor a red eye, and a blurred disc margin is not a glaucoma sign.\n- **C — central retinal artery occlusion.** CRAO drops vision **within seconds**, not over hours, and its fundus is the pale oedematous retina with a **cherry-red spot** and thread-like arterioles — `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` builds its Case 1 to exactly that diagnosis. Blurred disc *edges* are not what CRAO shows, and CRAO vision is usually far worse than 6/60.\n\n**The one thing the stem does not give you.** A relative afferent pupillary defect would clinch it, and papillitis is the form in which `L21` slide 8 marks RAPD with **three ticks**. The stem omits the pupil, so the diagnosis has to be made on laterality plus the acuity-versus-swelling mismatch alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A unilateral swollen disc with a marked acute drop in acuity is papillitis — the disc-swelling form of optic neuritis — because papilledema is bilateral and spares vision early.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q585), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-586', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'All are seen in **3rd nerve palsy** **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Ptosis','Diplopia','Miosis','Outwards eye deviation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The third nerve carries the parasympathetic supply to the sphincter pupillae, so losing it gives a big pupil, not a small one.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 2 draws the two supplies and labels them: parasympathetic → short ciliary nerve → **ACh** → **sphincter pupillae**; sympathetic → long ciliary nerve → **NE** → **dilator iridis**. Slide 4, *Mydriasis*, then lists under Neurologic: \"Parasympathetic paralysis: **3rd nerve paralysis**\". Miosis belongs to the opposite lesion — slide 3 files it under \"Sympathetic paralysis (Horner's syndrome)\" and \"Pontine hemorrhage\".\n\n**Why A, B and D are all present.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 lists the third nerve palsy in four lines and the options reproduce three of them:\n\n- **Ptosis** — the nerve supplies levator palpebrae superioris; the book notes it \"masks the diplopia\". `L30) Squint II` slides 11 and 12 photograph a lid dropped so completely that only the lashes show.\n- **Diplopia** — binocular, from the misalignment; it appears once the lid is lifted, which is why `L30` slide 12's second photograph is an examiner's finger raising the lid.\n- **Outward eye deviation** — \"The eye is turned outward and downward\", because the only two muscles still working are the lateral rectus (CN VI) and the superior oblique (CN IV). `L30` slide 11's bottom-right frame shows the eye beneath the lifted lid sitting *down and out*.\n\n**The pupil in practice.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 qualifies the mydriasis — \"Mydriasis (in compressive lesions e.g., aneurysm or tumour)\" — and its figure carries the rule as an algorithm: pupil involved → trauma, aneurysm, tumour; pupil spared → vasculopathy. The mechanism is drawn on the same page: the pupillary fibres \"lie dorsal and peripheral\" and are supplied by pial vessels, so an external compression takes them first, while an infarct of the vasa nervorum kills the core and spares them. So the pupil may be normal in a diabetic palsy — but it is **never small**, which is what the question asks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Third nerve palsy gives ptosis, binocular diplopia and a down-and-out eye, with a dilated or spared pupil — never miosis, which is the sign of the opposite (sympathetic) lesion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q586), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-587', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'Which is **NOT** found in **papilledema**?',
    options:['Blurred vision','Blurred margins of disc','Cupping of disc','Retinal edema'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A swollen disc has no cup; a cupped disc is not swollen. The two signs are mutually exclusive, and that is the whole question.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.187 lists the ophthalmoscopic signs of optic disc swelling and item 4 is \"**Absence of a central cup (obliterated cup)**\" — the swelling fills the excavation in. `L21) Optic Nerve` describes the same thing in every one of its swollen-disc photographs: on slide 5 the papilloedema panel reads \"Cup — not visible at all; filled in by the swelling\", and the AION panel beside it \"Cup — obliterated\".\n\n**Cupping belongs to glaucoma.** `L21` slide 10 photographs the glaucomatous disc and makes the contrast explicit: \"the disc outline itself is **sharp**; it is the **CUP**, not the margin, that is abnormal. This is the difference from every swollen disc above.\" So option C is not merely absent from papilledema — it is the sign of the disease papilledema is being distinguished from.\n\n**Why the other three are found.**\n\n- **A — blurred vision.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.188: \"transient visual obscuration or transient attacks of b[l]urred vision may be present\", and the chapter's comparison table gives papilledema \"Transient attacks of blurred vision\". Note the qualifier: it is transient and early vision is otherwise normal, which is what Q591 and Q609 test.\n- **B — blurred margins of disc.** The first of the book's five signs of disc swelling: \"Indistinct disc margins\". `L21` slide 5: \"Margins — COMPLETELY OBLITERATED; there is no line where the disc ends and the retina begins.\"\n- **D — retinal edema.** The oedema does not stop at the disc edge; the book's sign list includes \"Obscuration of retinal vessels near the nerve\" and haemorrhage \"on and surrounding the nerve head\", and `L21` slide 5 records the \"peripapillary nerve fibre layer hazy\". Peripapillary retinal oedema is part of the established picture.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Disc swelling obliterates the physiological cup, so cupping — the sign of glaucoma — is the one finding that cannot coexist with papilledema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q587), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-588', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'**Optic disc diameter** is:',
    options:['1 mm','1.5 mm','2 mm','3 mm'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**One disc diameter ≈ 1.5 mm, and it is a measuring unit before it is an anatomical fact.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.36 states it in the ophthalmoscopy section: \"Lesions seen with the ophthalmoscope can be measured in **disc diameters** (1 disc diameter equals approximately **1.5 mm**).\" That is why the number is taught at all — the fundus has no ruler on it, so the disc becomes the ruler, and lesion size is recorded as *two disc diameters from the fovea*, *half a disc diameter across*, and so on.\n\n**The rest of the normal disc, from the same page**, because the disc's other numbers are examined alongside this one: it is \"slightly **oval in the vertical meridian**\", **pink** from surface capillaries, with a **sharp** margin and a central whitish depression, the physiologic cup. The average **cup-to-disc ratio is 0.2–0.4**; a C/D over **0.5**, or a difference of more than **0.2** between the two eyes, is a referral threshold for glaucoma.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.** They are the plausible near-misses on either side. **1 mm** and **2 mm** bracket the true value and are the numbers a candidate reaches for when rounding; **3 mm** is roughly the diameter of the *macula*, not the disc, and is twice too large — a 3 mm disc would occupy an implausible share of the posterior pole. Neither `L21) Optic Nerve` nor `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` prints any dimension of the disc at all; `L21` slide 2 shows the normal disc as a photograph and describes only its colour, margins, cup and vessels.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'One optic disc diameter is about 1.5 mm, the unit in which fundus lesions are measured at the ophthalmoscope.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q588), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-589', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Optic nerve function** is best studied by:',
    options:['Direct Ophthalmoscope','Retinoscope','Perimetry','Gonioscopy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The disc tells you how the nerve head looks; the field tells you how the nerve works.**\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 3 is the only typed bullet list in the deck, and it prints the five manifestations of optic nerve dysfunction with icons that carry meaning:\n\n| Sign | The deck's icon |\n|---|---|\n| Visual acuity | ↓ (red down arrow) |\n| Colour vision | ↓ |\n| Contrast sensitivity | ↓ |\n| **Field defect** | ★ (red star) |\n| **RAPD** | ★ |\n\nThe two starred items are, in the deck's own arrangement, \"the two signs that **localise the lesion to the optic nerve**\". Of the four instruments offered, only perimetry measures either of them.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — direct ophthalmoscope.** It shows the optic *disc*, which is only the visible 1 mm of the nerve, and it can be entirely normal in a nerve that is functionally destroyed. Retrobulbar neuritis is the standing proof: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 tags it \"[normal optic disc]\" while the acuity, colour vision and field are all wrecked. `L21` slides 5–19 are almost all fundus photographs, and the deck still needs slides 3, 4 and 8 to say what the nerve is *doing*.\n- **B — retinoscope.** An objective refracting instrument. It measures the eye's refractive error, nothing about conduction in the nerve.\n- **D — gonioscopy.** A mirrored contact lens for viewing the **iridocorneal angle**, the test that separates open- from closed-angle glaucoma. It never looks at the posterior segment at all.\n\n**What perimetry is, in this course.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.23 lists automated perimetry as \"an accurate quantitative test that can be used for assessment and follow-up\", beside confrontation, light projection and the Amsler grid. Note the measured limit of the lecture material here: `L21`'s own register records that no field-testing method is described anywhere in its twenty slides — slide 4 draws five defects by hand and slide 9 shows one greyscale printout, and that is the whole of it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Perimetry maps the visual field, one of the two signs that localise a lesion to the optic nerve, and it detects dysfunction that leaves the disc looking normal.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q589), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-590', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Optic nerve axon** emerges from:',
    options:['Ganglion cells','Rods and cones','Amacrine cells','Inner nuclear layer'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The optic nerve is a bundle of ganglion cell axons and nothing else.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.187 opens the optic nerve part of the chapter with the sentence: \"The optic nerve consists of the **axons of the ganglion cells of the retina**, as they converge to create the nerve, they pass through the **lamina cribrosa** of the sclera creating the optic nerve.\" p.12 says the same from the fundus side: the optic disc \"is composed of axons whose cell bodies lie in the **ganglion cell layer** of the retina\".\n\n**The chain the other options sit in.** The retina's through-path is three neurones deep: **photoreceptor → bipolar cell → ganglion cell**, and only the third one sends its axon out of the eye.\n\n- **B — rods and cones.** These are the *first* neurone, the photoreceptors. Their processes end in the outer plexiform layer on bipolar cells; nothing of theirs leaves the retina. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 7 labels the inset retinal diagram \"Rod Cell · Cone Cell · Bipolar Cell · **Ganglion Cell** · **Axons** · Optic Nerve\" in that order, and the label *Axons* sits on the ganglion cell, not the photoreceptor.\n- **C — amacrine cells.** Lateral interneurones of the inner plexiform layer. They modulate the bipolar-to-ganglion synapse and project nowhere outside the retina.\n- **D — inner nuclear layer.** This is the layer of cell bodies of the bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells. It is a *layer*, and it is the wrong one — the ganglion cell bodies lie in their own layer, internal to it, with the nerve fibre layer internal again.\n\n**Why the fact matters clinically.** Because the nerve is made of ganglion cell axons, anything that kills ganglion cells eventually shows as optic atrophy — which is why `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 can define optic atrophy as \"Degeneration of the optic nerve at any part starting from the **retina** to the lateral geniculate body\". It is also why glaucoma, a disease of the ganglion cell axons at the lamina cribrosa, produces disc changes rather than retinal ones.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The optic nerve is formed of the axons of the retinal ganglion cells, which converge at the disc and leave the eye through the lamina cribrosa.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q590), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-591', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Papilledema** has all the following characteristics **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Marked loss of vision','Blurring of disc margins','Hyperemia of disc','Field defect'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Papilledema is the disease in which the disc looks terrible and the vision is fine — that dissociation is the sign, and it is what option A destroys.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 states it twice. In the N.B box: \"**Vision is not affected early in the condition** but transient visual obscuration or transient attacks of b[l]urred vision may be present.\" And in the p.189 comparison table, under Symptoms: papilledema — \"Visual acuity usually normal · Transient attacks of blurred vision · + symptoms of increased ICP (headache & vomiting)\"; papillitis — \"**Marked visual deterioration**\". `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8's handwritten table reduces it to one character: VA is \"**N**\" for papilledema and \"**↓**\" for papillitis.\n\nThe Tutorial timeline on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 gives the full arc, and it is the reason the word *early* matters: \"Early: no visual symptoms · Later: transient visual loss for few seconds · Latest: significant visual loss (optic atrophy)\". So marked loss of vision is not impossible in papilledema — it is the **end stage**, after chronic swelling has become secondary optic atrophy — but it is not a characteristic of the condition, and the same page warns that \"Chronic papilledema can cause permanent optic nerve damage and blindness if not treated\".\n\n**Why the other three are characteristic.**\n\n- **B — blurring of disc margins.** The first item on the book's list of the ophthalmoscopic signs of disc swelling (p.187): \"Indistinct disc margins\".\n- **C — hyperaemia of the disc.** Item 1 of the p.188 box, \"Hyperaemia of the disc\", from the dilated surface capillaries. `L21` slide 5 photographs it as \"a hot red-orange\" disc.\n- **D — field defect.** Item 6 of the same box: \"Visual field examination shows → **enlargement of the blind spot**\". An enlarged blind spot *is* a field defect — and, crucially, one the patient does not notice, which is exactly why the vision can be normal while the field is abnormal.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Papilledema swells and reddens the disc and enlarges the blind spot while central acuity stays normal early; marked visual loss belongs to papillitis or to end-stage chronic papilledema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.93 (Q591), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-592', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Homonymous hemianopia** is the result of a lesion in:',
    options:['Optic chiasma','Retina','Optic tract','Optic nerve'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Homonymous means the same half of the field is lost in both eyes, and that can only happen once the two eyes' fibres have been sorted by side — which happens at the chiasm and is complete in the tract.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 defines the word and then localises it: \"**Homonymous hemianopia** — loss of either the right or the left half of the visual field in both eyes\", and \"Damage to the **optic tract**, the lateral geniculate body, the optic radiation, or the occipital cortex induces homonymous hemianopia.\"\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` teaches it station by station and draws the tract lesion twice. Slide 17 (*Optic Tract*) carries the red caption \"**Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (incongruous)**\" and a field figure in which the **left half of both circles** is solid black. Slide 24's summary lesion-map does the same with the sides labelled: lesion **C** across the right optic tract gives \"Left field: LEFT half black · Right field: LEFT half black = LEFT HOMONYMOUS HEMIANOPIA\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — optic chiasma.** At the chiasm the fibres are sorted by *retinal half*, not by side of space: the crossing nasal fibres of both eyes are what a midline lesion takes, giving a **bitemporal** (heteronymous) hemianopia. `L22` slide 15 captions it \"Bitemporal Hemianopia\" and slide 24 row 2 draws the left field's left half and the right field's right half black — opposite halves, therefore not homonymous.\n- **B — retina.** A retinal lesion affects **one eye only**; it cannot produce a matching defect in the fellow eye. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 opens the part by noting that lesions \"from the retina to the occipital lobes\" produce field defects, but the monocular ones stop at the chiasm.\n- **D — optic nerve.** Also monocular. `L22` slide 14 draws the optic nerve lesion as one circle entirely clear and the other entirely black — total blindness of one eye — and slide 24 row 1 confirms it, \"Right field: entirely BLACK — TOTAL BLINDNESS of the right eye\", with the left field completely normal.\n\n**The refinement the option list does not test.** Congruity increases the further back the lesion sits: the tract gives an *incongruous* defect (`L22` draws its two black halves deliberately unequal), the occipital cortex a *congruous* one with macular sparing (slide 23 draws them identical, with a notch cut at the centre). `L22`'s own register records that the rule behind this is drawn and never written down anywhere in the deck.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A homonymous hemianopia localises behind the chiasm — classically to the optic tract — because only there are the fibres of both eyes grouped by side of visual space.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q592), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-593', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Mydriasis** is present in all the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Third nerve lesion','Pontine hemorrhage','Datura poisoning','Fourth stage of anesthesia'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Pontine haemorrhage is the classic pin-point pupil, and it is the only option on the list that constricts.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` splits the causes across two facing slides, and the answer is a matter of reading the right one. Slide 3, **Miosis**, prints under Neurologic: \"Sympathetic paralysis (Horner's syndrome)\" and \"**Pontine hemorrhage**\". `ophthalmology.pdf` p.179 files it the same way, in the Small Pupil column under the sub-heading \"**Pin-point Pupil**: Morphine use · Organophosphorus poisoning · **Pontine haemorrhage**\".\n\nThe mechanism is that the descending sympathetic pathway runs through the pons on its way from the hypothalamus to the cord. A pontine bleed cuts it bilaterally, leaving the parasympathetic sphincter unopposed — pin-point pupils that still react, though the reaction is too small to see without magnification. (*That the pupils remain reactive is* **not taken from the course material**; the sources give the sign, not the physiology.)\n\n**Why the other three dilate.** `L22` slide 4, **Mydriasis**:\n\n| Option | Where the deck files it |\n|---|---|\n| **A** Third nerve lesion | Neurologic → \"Parasympathetic paralysis: **3rd nerve paralysis**\" |\n| **C** Datura poisoning | Pharmacologic → \"**Parasympatholytics**\" |\n| **D** Fourth stage of anesthesia | Anaesthesia → \"**2nd, 4th stages**\" |\n\nThe anaesthesia line is worth learning as a pattern rather than a list: stages **II and IV dilate, stage III constricts**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.179 prints the same split across its two columns — \"Stage II , IV anaesthesia\" under the dilated pupil, \"Stage III anaesthesia\" under the small one — and its dilated column ends with \"Death (bilateral)\", which is what stage IV is.\n\n**⚠️ Datura is not in the course material.** Grepped case-insensitively across all twenty-nine cached ophthalmology lecture decks and all twelve cached book chapters: **zero hits**. What the sources give is the drug *class* — `L22` slide 4's \"Parasympatholytics\". *Datura stramonium* (thorn apple, jimsonweed) contains atropine, hyoscyamine and scopolamine, which are exactly that class, and accidental contact with the plant is a classical cause of a unilateral fixed dilated pupil in a well child or a gardener. That identification is **not taken from the course material** and is supplied here so the distractor is not left unexplained.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Third nerve palsy, antimuscarinic (datura) poisoning and stages II and IV of anaesthesia all dilate the pupil; pontine haemorrhage is the pin-point pupil, from interruption of the descending sympathetic pathway.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q593), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-594', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'In **complete third nerve paralysis** the direction of the affected eye **in the primary position** is:',
    options:['Inward','Outward','Outward and up','Outward and down'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Take away everything the third nerve supplies and two muscles are left pulling: the lateral rectus abducts and the superior oblique depresses. The eye sits down and out.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 states it in one line — \"**The eye is turned outward and downward**\" — and its figure carries the same words as a call-out, \"**Down and out** — Unopposed left superior oblique and lateral rectus muscles\". The muscle map on the same page gives the arithmetic: CN III supplies the medial rectus, superior rectus, inferior rectus and inferior oblique; CN VI supplies the lateral rectus and CN IV the superior oblique. Four of the six go, two remain, and both of the survivors pull the eye away from the nose — one horizontally, one downward.\n\n`L30) Squint II` photographs it twice without ever captioning it. Slide 11's bottom-right frame shows the examiner's finger lifting the ptotic lid, and \"the eye beneath is revealed sitting **DOWN AND OUT**: its iris is displaced toward the outer canthus and low in the fissure, with a large area of white sclera above and nasal to it\". Slide 12 repeats the manoeuvre on a second patient and adds the dilated pupil.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — inward.** That is the resting position in a **sixth** nerve palsy: the medial rectus is unopposed and the eye turns in. `L30) Squint II` slide 15 draws it as a two-eye cartoon and photographs the esotropia in its primary-position frame.\n- **B — outward.** Half the answer. Abduction alone would be the picture if only the medial rectus were paralysed; but a *complete* third nerve palsy also removes the superior rectus and inferior oblique, the two elevators, so the eye drops as well. This is exactly the answer the bank keys at **Q620**, where this same question is reprinted — see the note below.\n- **C — outward and up.** The wrong vertical. Elevation is lost, not gained: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 lists \"Loss of elevation and adduction\", and `L30` slide 11's top-right frame shows the affected eye elevating far less than its fellow.\n\n**⚠️ The bank prints this question twice and keys it two different ways.** `opqb-t14-620` on p.97 carries the identical stem and the identical four options, and its key on p.101 is **B, \"Outward\"**. Both letters were read from enlarged crops of the printed answer pages. The key recorded on each entry is the one the bank printed for that question; the discrepancy is noted, not resolved, and every source in the course — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 and `L30` slides 11 and 12 — supports *down and out*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In a complete third nerve palsy only the lateral rectus and superior oblique still act, so the eye rests down and out — the same question is reprinted at Q620 with a different printed key.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q594), key p.100; reprinted as Q620 on p.97 with key B' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-595', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Retrobulbar neuritis** is characterized by:',
    options:['May be acute or chronic','Shows central or paracentral scotoma for red & green','Inflammation of optic nerves behind the eye','May be due to disseminated sclerosis','All of the above'],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**All four statements are printed in the course material, so the answer is E.**\n\n**A — may be acute or chronic.** `ophthalmology.pdf` treats them as two separate numbered topics. Acute: \"(2) Optic Neuritis\", classified into papillitis, retrobulbar neuritis and neuroretinitis, p.190. Chronic: \"(3) **Chronic Retrobulbar Neuritis** — also referred to as nutritional or toxic optic neuropathy\", same page, \"usually bilateral\", from vitamin B12 deficiency, tobacco–alcohol amblyopia or drugs such as ethambutol and chloramphenicol. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 makes the same split in the lecturer's handwriting, giving \"Acute optic neuritis\" a central scotoma and \"**Chronic RB neuritis**\" a centrocecal one.\n\n**B — central or paracentral scotoma for red & green.** The scotoma is printed everywhere: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191 gives the field defect as \"usually central scotoma but may be centrocecal, altitudinal or arcuate\", and p.189's comparison table gives papillitis \"Central scotoma\". The **red-green** part is the colour-vision loss that runs with it — \"Impaired colour vision\" is item 3 of the book's signs of optic nerve dysfunction (p.187) and appears again in the retrobulbar signs list (p.190). *That the earliest colour loss in optic neuritis is in the red-green axis, and that a scotoma is often detectable to a red target before a white one, is* **not taken from the course material**; the sources pair the scotoma and the colour defect without specifying the axis.\n\n**C — inflammation of optic nerves behind the eye.** The definition. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190: optic neuritis is \"Inflammation and demyelination of the optic nerve\", and the retrobulbar form is the one with a \"[normal optic disc]\" — normal precisely because the inflamed segment lies behind the globe where the ophthalmoscope cannot reach it.\n\n**D — may be due to disseminated sclerosis.** *Disseminated sclerosis* is the older name for **multiple sclerosis**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 lists it first among the causes and calls it \"the most common cause\", with the figure that Q603's printed box repeats: \"**50 %** of patients presenting with isolated optic neuritis will ultimately develop multiple sclerosis\". `L21` slide 8's handwritten table gives papillitis the etiology \"**MS**, viral, toxic, nubition[al]\".\n\n**Reading the option list.** Four true statements and an \"all of the above\" is a question about completeness, not discrimination — the trap is stopping at the first statement that looks incomplete (A and B both sound like partial truths) and choosing it instead of reading on.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retrobulbar neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve behind the globe, acute or chronic, commonly demyelinating, producing a central or centrocaecal scotoma with impaired colour vision and a normal-looking disc.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q595), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-596', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Primary optic atrophy** is characterized by',
    options:['Optic disc is white in color','Lamina cribrosa is well seen','Retina & blood vessels are normal','All of the above'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three descriptions of the same photograph, so the answer is D.**\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14 prints four fundus photographs with the four type names burned into them and no definitions at all; the *Primary* panel is described in the deck's fixed order and answers all three options at once:\n\n| The bank's option | What `L21` slide 14's Primary panel shows |\n|---|---|\n| **A** Disc is white | \"a **CHALKY WHITE** disc, dead white against a red fundus\" |\n| **B** Lamina cribrosa well seen | \"the pale disc is **flat**, with the cup not distinguishable from the surrounding pallor\" — the floor of the disc is exposed |\n| **C** Retina & vessels normal | \"**Vessels — normal in calibre** … Surrounding retina — **normal red-orange, no peripapillary change**\" |\n\nSlide 15 shows a second, uncaptioned example and the deck's note reads it back against slide 14: \"pale disc, sharp margins, normal vessels, normal retina\" — the primary pattern.\n\n**Why each option matters as a discriminator, not just a description.**\n\n- **A** is shared with every type — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 says \"The optic nerve appears **pale in all types** of optic atrophy due to decreased vascularity\" — so whiteness alone proves nothing. It is the *quality* of the white that differs: chalky in primary, dirty grey in secondary, waxy yellow in consecutive.\n- **B** separates primary from **secondary**. In secondary atrophy the disc substance is replaced by glial tissue left behind by the swelling, the margins stay indistinct and the lamina is buried — `L21` slide 14's Secondary panel reads \"Margins — **INDISTINCT AND BLURRED**, the disc edge fading into a fuzzy halo … Cup — filled in and not identifiable\".\n- **C** separates primary from **consecutive**. Consecutive atrophy is caused by retinal disease, so the retina cannot be normal: the same slide's Consecutive panel shows \"**MARKEDLY ATTENUATED**\" thread-like arterioles on a washed-out retina, and slide 16 shows the cause itself — coarse peripheral bone-spicule pigment.\n\n**A note on the exposed lamina.** The lamina cribrosa is the sieve-like scleral plate the ganglion cell axons pass through (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.187). When the axons in front of it have withered and no swelling has intervened, its pores become visible at the disc floor as fine grey stippling. `L21` slide 11's line diagram draws \"a row of small BLACK DOTS … across the cup floor (the pores of the lamina cribrosa)\" — though it draws them in the glaucomatous disc, not the atrophic one, and no slide in the deck states the sign in words.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Primary optic atrophy shows a chalky white, flat disc with sharp margins and a visible lamina cribrosa, on a retina whose vessels and background are entirely normal.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q596), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-597', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Traumatic optic neuropathy** treated by:',
    options:['Systemic steroids','Antibiotics','Anti-histaminic','None of the above'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ This is a gap in the course material, filled and tagged.** Traumatic optic neuropathy appears in exactly two places in the whole cached ophthalmology corpus and neither states a treatment: `L25) Trauma I` names it once in a list — \"ORBITAL ROOF and apex fractures, and traumatic optic neuropathy\" — and `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.3 mentions it only to hand it on, \"(Refer to trauma chapter)\". The neuro-ophthalmology chapter's own not-covered register lists it as absent. Everything below the first line is therefore **not taken from the course material** unless a citation is given.\n\n**What the injury is.** A closed head or orbital injury — classically a blow to the brow or the lateral orbital rim — transmits force to the optic canal, where the nerve is tethered in bone and cannot move. The result is contusion, oedema within a rigid canal, and secondary ischaemia of a nerve that has no collateral supply. Vision falls immediately or over hours, there is a **relative afferent pupillary defect**, and the disc looks entirely normal for the first four to six weeks because the injury is retrobulbar — it pales only later. The RAPD is the finding that makes the diagnosis in an unconscious patient, and it is the one sign the course does teach in this context: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187's box lists RAPD second among the signs of optic nerve dysfunction.\n\n**Why systemic steroids is the keyed answer.** The rationale is anti-oedema — reduce swelling inside the unyielding canal and the compressive component of the ischaemia falls. High-dose systemic corticosteroid has long been the conventional first move, alongside surgical optic canal decompression in selected cases. *(The evidence for both is weak and modern practice in many centres is observation; that qualification is* **not taken from the course material***.)* Note that steroid is also the course's answer for the other acute optic neuropathies it does treat — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191 gives IV methylprednisolone for demyelinating optic neuritis and p.192 \"High dose of systemic steroid\" for arteritic AION — so the keyed option is at least consistent with how this book treats an acutely inflamed or swollen nerve.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **B — antibiotics.** There is no infection. Antibiotics belong to orbital cellulitis or a penetrating injury with a retained organic foreign body, neither of which is described here.\n- **C — anti-histaminic.** Antihistamines treat type I hypersensitivity — allergic conjunctivitis and the like. They have no action on nerve oedema and no role in any optic neuropathy.\n- **D — none of the above.** Defensible if one holds that no treatment is proven, but it is not the key; the bank is testing the conventional teaching, not the trial evidence.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Traumatic optic neuropathy is a compressive-ischaemic injury of the nerve in the optic canal presenting with an RAPD and a normal disc, and the conventional first-line treatment taught here is high-dose systemic steroid.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q597), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-598', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:"**Horner's syndrome** is characterized by all of the following **EXCEPT**:",
    options:['Miosis','Widened palpebral fissure','Slight enophthalmos','Unilateral absence of sweating'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Horner's syndrome loses the sympathetic supply, so the lid falls and the fissure NARROWS. Widening is the opposite sign.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 11 lists the syndrome in six words: \"Miosis · Dilation Lag · **Ptosis** · Apparent enophthalmos · Anhydrosis · Heterochromia (Congenital)\". `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 prints the same set: \"Miosis (with anisocoria more in the dark) · **Ptosis** and anhidrosis of the ipsilateral face and neck · Enophthalmos · Heterochromia of the iris in congenital cases.\"\n\n**Which branch produces which sign** — the book's sympathetic-pathway figure on p.180 is the only place this is spelled out, and it maps each sign onto a lost branch:\n\n| Sign | Lost branch |\n|---|---|\n| **Miosis** | nerve to the **dilator pupillae** |\n| **Ptosis** | nerve to the tarsal / **Müller's muscle**, which \"keeps eyelid elevated\" |\n| **Anhidrosis** | nerve to the **sweat gland** |\n\nMüller's muscle is a small smooth-muscle sheet in the upper lid that adds roughly 2 mm of elevation on top of the levator's action; lose it and the lid drops by about that much. The lower lid has its partner, the inferior tarsal muscle, which loses its downward pull and rides **up**. The two together narrow the fissure from both directions — and that is why the eye only *looks* sunken.\n\n**Why the other three are correct findings.**\n\n- **A — miosis.** The defining pupil sign, and the reason `ophthalmology.pdf` p.179 files Horner in its Small Pupil column. `L22` slide 11's photograph shows it beside a normal fellow eye.\n- **C — slight enophthalmos.** Printed by both sources, and both hedge it — `L22` writes \"**Apparent** enophthalmos\", and the box printed under Q626 of this same topic explains it: \"Apparent enophthalmos → caused by the combined upper and lower lid ptosis\". The globe has not moved.\n- **D — unilateral absence of sweating.** Anhidrosis of the ipsilateral face and neck. Its extent localises the lesion, because the sudomotor fibres to the face leave the sympathetic chain at the carotid bifurcation — though the course does not teach that localisation logic; `ophthalmology.pdf`'s register records that the first/second/third-order division \"appear[s] in a figure label only\".\n\n**The sign the bank does not offer.** *Dilation lag* — the affected pupil takes several seconds longer than its fellow to redilate when the light is removed, which is why the anisocoria is greater in the dark. `L22` slide 11 names it and slide 12 photographs the light-versus-dark comparison that demonstrates it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Horner syndrome is miosis, ptosis from loss of Müller's muscle, apparent enophthalmos and ipsilateral anhidrosis — the palpebral fissure narrows, never widens.",
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q598), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-599', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'A **right optic tract lesion** results in:',
    options:['Right homonymous hemianopia.','Left homonymous hemianopia.','Bitemporal hemianopia.','Binasal hemianopia.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Contralateral. The right tract carries the left half of the world, so a right tract lesion blinds the left field of both eyes.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 24 is the deck's summary lesion-map and it draws this exact lesion with its sides labelled. Lesion **C** is \"across the optic tract on the RIGHT of the picture\", and its row of field circles reads: \"Left field: LEFT half black, right half grey · Right field: LEFT half black, right half grey = **LEFT HOMONYMOUS HEMIANOPIA** (same side lost in both eyes, contralateral to the right-sided lesion). Complete, no sparing.\" Slide 17 says it in words as a red caption — \"**Contralateral** homonymous hemianopia (incongruous)\".\n\n**Why it is the opposite side.** Each half of the brain handles the opposite half of space, and the chiasm is where that regrouping happens. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 prints the legend under its pathway figure: \"Objects in the **right** half of the visual field form images in the **left** half of each retina and are transmitted to the **left** hemisphere.\" Run it the other way for this question: the right tract receives the temporal fibres of the right eye and the crossed nasal fibres of the left eye, and those two sets look at the **left** half of space.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — right homonymous hemianopia.** The correct defect for a **left**-sided lesion. This is the single commonest error in the topic, and it is a sidedness slip rather than a knowledge gap.\n- **C — bitemporal hemianopia.** A **midline chiasmal** lesion, which takes the crossing nasal fibres of both eyes. `L22` slide 15 captions it and slide 24 row 2 draws it: left field's LEFT half black, right field's RIGHT half black — opposite halves, so heteronymous, not homonymous.\n- **D — binasal hemianopia.** The rarity: **bilateral lateral** compression of the chiasm, taking the uncrossed temporal fibres of both eyes. `L22` slide 16 is the only slide on it and it labels its circles \"LE\" and \"RE\" — the deck's clearest statement of its own field convention.\n\n**What the option list does not test.** *Incongruity.* `L22` draws the tract lesion's two black halves deliberately unequal, because the fibres from the two eyes are not yet fully paired at that level; a lesion further back gives a congruous defect. The deck's register records that this rule is drawn and never written.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A right optic tract lesion produces a left (contralateral) homonymous hemianopia, because each tract carries the opposite half of visual space from both eyes.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q599), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-600', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Bitemporal hemianopia** can be due to:',
    options:['Craniopharyngioma.','Frontal lobe meningioma.','Cerebellar astrocytoma.','Large sized occipital lobe tumor.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Bitemporal hemianopia is a lesion AT THE MIDLINE OF THE CHIASM, so the only acceptable option is the tumour that sits there.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 gives the causes in one line: \"Optic chiasm lesions: … Bitemporal hemianopia caused by **pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma, or parasellar meningioma**.\" Craniopharyngioma is the option printed, and it is the second of the book's three.\n\n**Why the midline matters.** The temporal half of each eye's field is carried by the **nasal** retinal fibres, and those are the fibres that cross. A lesion pressing on the middle of the chiasm from below or above takes both crossing bundles and spares the uncrossed temporal fibres, so both eyes lose their outer field. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 15 draws it and captions it *Bitemporal Hemianopia*: two circles, the left one black on its LEFT half and the right one black on its RIGHT half — opposite halves, which is what *heteronymous* means. Slide 16 draws the mirror lesion, **bilateral lateral** chiasmal compression, and captions it *Binasal Hemianopia*.\n\n**Why the other three cannot do it.**\n\n- **B — frontal lobe meningioma.** The frontal lobe is not on the visual pathway, and the book's meningioma is specifically a **parasellar** one, i.e. one growing beside the sella and reaching the chiasm. A tumour confined to the frontal convexity produces no field defect at all until it raises the intracranial pressure. (A meningioma of the olfactory groove or sphenoid ridge can reach the optic nerve, which is how Foster Kennedy syndrome arises — `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8 names Foster Kennedy once, as the exception to papilledema's bilaterality, and never explains it. Even then the defect is a unilateral optic-nerve one, not a bitemporal loss.)\n- **C — cerebellar astrocytoma.** Posterior fossa, nowhere near the pathway. It affects the eye by obstructing CSF flow and raising intracranial pressure, and its field sign is the **enlarged blind spot** of papilledema (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.188, `L21` slide 8).\n- **D — large occipital lobe tumour.** A lesion here is as far behind the chiasm as the pathway goes, and everything behind the chiasm gives a **homonymous** defect — the same side lost in both eyes. `L22` slide 23 captions the occipital lesion \"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (congruous with macular sparing)\". Homonymous and heteronymous are mutually exclusive.\n\n**The rule the option list is built on:** in front of the chiasm the defect is **monocular**, at the chiasm it is **heteronymous**, behind the chiasm it is **homonymous**. `L22` slide 24 prints all three on one figure, row by row.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Bitemporal hemianopia localises to the midline of the optic chiasm, where the crossing nasal fibres of both eyes run — pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma or parasellar meningioma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q600), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-601', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'A **77-year-old diabetic & hypertensive** male patient notices that he **cannot see on his left-hand side**. An MRI brain confirms that he has a **right occipital cortical infarct**. Visual field examination may show presence of:',
    options:['Congruous left hemianopia.','Incongruous left hemianopia.','Constricted visual field.','Altitudinal visual field defect.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The further back along the pathway a lesion sits, the more closely the two eyes' defects match each other. The occipital cortex is the back of the pathway, so the defect is congruous.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 23 is this question. Its red caption reads \"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia **(congruous with macular sparing)**\", and its field figure draws two circles whose left halves are black and **drawn identical to each other**, each with a small rounded notch left white at the very centre. That identity is the deck's way of writing *congruous*, and the notch is macular sparing.\n\n**Why congruity increases posteriorly.** Fibres from corresponding points of the two retinas start out apart and are progressively paired as the pathway runs back, being fully matched only in the striate cortex. So:\n\n| Lesion site | Deck's own caption | Congruity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Optic tract | `L22` slide 17 | \"incongruous\" — the two black halves drawn deliberately **unequal** |\n| Lateral geniculate body | `L22` slide 19 | \"incongruous\" — same caption, same unequal halves |\n| Occipital cortex | `L22` slide 23 | \"**congruous** with macular sparing\" — halves drawn identical |\n\nSlide 24's summary figure sets rows 3, 4 and 5 against each other to make the same point in one picture.\n\n**Why \"left\".** Each hemisphere sees the **opposite** half of space. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 prints the legend under its pathway figure: \"Objects in the right half of the visual field form images in the left half of each retina and are transmitted to the **left** hemisphere.\" A **right** occipital infarct therefore takes the **left** half of the field of both eyes, which is exactly the complaint in the stem — *he cannot see on his left-hand side*.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **B — incongruous left hemianopia.** Correct side, wrong congruity, and the option exists to punish a reader who stops at *homonymous*. Incongruity points **forward** to the tract or the geniculate body, not back to the cortex.\n- **C — constricted visual field.** A concentric loss from the periphery inward. It belongs to retinal and glaucomatous disease, and to functional visual loss; it respects no vertical midline, which is the signature every post-chiasmal defect must carry.\n- **D — altitudinal defect.** Loss of the upper or lower half, respecting the **horizontal** meridian instead of the vertical one. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 assigns it to AION, and slide 9's greyscale printout shows a dense superior altitudinal loss stepping down to a flat horizontal edge. A horizontal cut-off is an optic-nerve or retinal-vascular sign, never a cortical one — the stem's diabetes and hypertension are there to make this distractor tempting.\n\n**What the stem does not test.** *Why* the macula is spared. `ophthalmology.pdf` labels macular sparing in a figure and its own register records that the dual blood supply of the occipital pole, which explains it, is absent from the chapter.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An occipital cortical lesion gives a contralateral homonymous hemianopia that is congruous and typically macula-sparing, because fibre pairing is complete only at the striate cortex.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.94 (Q601), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-602', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Optic atrophy** may be seen in all of the following conditions **EXCEPT**',
    options:['Retinitis pigmentosa.','Poor nutrition.','Toxic eye diseases.','Diseases of the visual cortex.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The book defines optic atrophy by where it can happen, and the definition is what answers this: it stops at the lateral geniculate body.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 opens the section with the sentence the question is built on: \"Degeneration of the optic nerve at any part starting **from the retina to the lateral geniculate body** is called optic atrophy.\" Everything behind the geniculate body — the optic radiation and the visual cortex — belongs to the **next** neurone in the chain. Killing that neurone cannot make the retinal ganglion cell axons in front of it degenerate, so a cortical lesion produces a dense field defect with a **normal-looking disc**.\n\n**Why the other three do produce it.**\n\n| Option | The type it produces | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** Retinitis pigmentosa | **Consecutive** optic atrophy — the book's own named example | p.194 |\n| **B** Poor nutrition | Chronic retrobulbar neuritis, \"also referred to as **nutritional** or toxic optic neuropathy\"; vitamin B12 deficiency is its first listed cause | p.190 |\n| **C** Toxic eye diseases | The same entity from the other side — tobacco–alcohol amblyopia, ethambutol, chloramphenicol | p.190 |\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8's handwritten table gives papillitis the etiology \"MS, viral, **toxic**, nubition[al]\" in the lecturer's own contraction, so the deck carries B and C too. Slide 16 shows the consecutive picture of A with its cause visible in the same photograph: a waxy pale disc, grossly attenuated vessels, and coarse black bone-spicule pigment through the mid-periphery.\n\n**The one fact common to all four types**, worth carrying out of this question: \"The optic nerve appears pale in **all** types of optic atrophy due to decreased vascularity\", and \"There is **no treatment** for optic atrophy\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.194).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Optic atrophy is degeneration anywhere from the retina to the lateral geniculate body, so retinal, nutritional and toxic disease all cause it while a visual cortex lesion cannot.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q602), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-603', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'The **most common cause of acute optic neuritis** in middle aged adults between **20 to 40 years** of age:',
    options:['Tobacco amblyopia.','Intake of antibiotics.','Demyelinating disorders.','Viral infections.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Etiology of ON:\n\n1. **Multiple sclerosis (The Most Common Cause):**\n   a. 50% of patients presenting with isolated optic neuritis will ultimately develop multiple sclerosis.\n   b. Characterized by remissions and relapses\n   c. All patients with optic neuritis should have an MRI with and without contrast.\n2. Idiopathic\n3. Post viral infections.\n4. Systemic conditions (e.g., syphilis, sarcoidosis, collagen vascular disease, human immunodeficiency virus).\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.100. It is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190's aetiology list copied verbatim, the 50 % figure included.)*\n\n**The age band in the stem is the corroborating evidence.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 gives the epidemiology as \"Most common in women ages **15 - 45**\", and the Tutorial box on p.191 narrows it further for the demyelinating form: \"Common in female · Age **20-40** years old (mean around 30 years)\". The stem's 20-to-40 is that line.\n\n**Why the distractors fail.**\n\n- **A — tobacco amblyopia.** A real optic neuropathy, but the wrong one: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 files \"Tobacco – Alcohol amblyopia\" under **chronic retrobulbar neuritis**, which is *bilateral* and *insidious*, not acute. Its field defect is the centrocaecal scotoma that `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 draws as a long oval fused to the blind spot.\n- **B — intake of antibiotics.** Drugs do damage the nerve, but the named ones sit in the same chronic, toxic group — \"ethambutol, chloramphenicol, etc.\" (p.190). Neither is common, and neither presents acutely.\n- **D — viral infections.** Printed in the box itself, as cause **3**, \"Post viral infections\" — real, and outranked. It is also the mechanism the bank invokes two questions later, in Q604's own box, where an attack of influenza is called \"Para infectious optic neuritis, **a rare case**\". The bank's two boxes therefore answer each other: viral is a cause, demyelination is *the* cause.\n\n**What follows from the key clinically.** Because half of these patients convert, the diagnosis of optic neuritis is a neurological event as much as an ocular one — hence the box's line c, an MRI with and without contrast in every case, and the Tutorial's \"MRI (plaques of demyelination)\" on p.191.",
    objective:'Demyelinating disease — multiple sclerosis — is the commonest cause of acute optic neuritis in the 20-to-40 age band, and half of isolated cases go on to develop MS.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q603), key + explanation p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-604', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'After an attack of **influenza**, a patient complained of **rapid diminution of vision in his right eye**. On examination, visual acuity was **6/60**, the cornea was clear, the **fundus was normal**, pupillary light reflex was **sluggish & ill-sustained**. To reach the diagnosis we should do:',
    options:['Ultrasonography','Field examination','Gonioscopy','Retinoscopy','tonometry'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"This is probably a case of retrobulbar neuritis which is caused by the viral attack called Para infectious optic neuritis, a rare case. It is retrobulbar because the fundus is normal, and optic neuritis because signs of optic neuropathy are seen (decreased vision& sluggish light reflex). Other signs of optic neuropathy are color vision affection & field changes\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.100. Its closing clause is the reason the answer is B: the box names field changes as the remaining sign of optic neuropathy, so the field is the test that completes the picture.)*\n\n**What the stem has already established, sign by sign.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187's Tutorial box lists four signs of optic nerve dysfunction — reduced acuity, a relative afferent pupillary defect, impaired colour vision, and a field defect. The vignette hands over two of them (6/60, a sluggish and ill-sustained light reflex) and rules the disc out by saying the fundus is normal, which is the definition of the **retrobulbar** form: p.190 tags it \"2. Retrobulbar neuritis **[normal optic disc]**\". Two of the four signs remain untested, and only one of them is on the option list.\n\n**Which field defect is expected.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 — the lecturer's handwritten page — draws it: under *Acute optic neuritis (central scotoma)* he sketches a hatched oval at the centre of the field with the blind spot beside it for scale. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191 adds that it is \"usually central scotoma but may be centrocecal, altitudinal or arcuate\".\n\n**Why the other four tests answer a question nobody asked.**\n\n- **A — ultrasonography.** Images the globe and orbit behind an opaque medium. The media here are explicitly clear, and B-scan cannot show optic nerve function.\n- **C — gonioscopy.** Views the drainage angle. It is the test for angle closure, and this eye is white, painless and normotensive by implication.\n- **D — retinoscopy.** Measures refractive error. A refractive cause cannot produce a sluggish pupil, and 6/60 from refraction alone would improve with a pinhole.\n- **E — tonometry.** Measures intraocular pressure. Nothing in the stem suggests glaucoma, and a normal pressure would exclude nothing.\n\n**The bank's own qualifier, worth keeping.** The box calls the para-infectious form \"a rare case\" — consistent with Q603 two entries earlier, where the same bank keys demyelinating disease as the commonest cause and prints post-viral infection third on its list.",
    objective:'In a suspected retrobulbar neuritis with reduced acuity, a sluggish pupil and a normal fundus, visual field examination is the test that completes the picture of optic nerve dysfunction.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q604), key + explanation p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-605', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'One of the following causes **sudden loss of vision**:',
    options:['CRVO','Retinal detachment','Vitreous hemorrhage','Amaurosis fugax'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Amaurosis fugax is a temporary sudden loss of vision that lasts typically only a few minutes and returns back to normal. It could precede CRAO & is similar to a transient ischemic attack of the brain, both are serious indicators of a possible stroke in the future; referral to neurology to manage this could be lifesaving\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.100.)*\n\n**⚠️ All four options cause sudden visual loss, and the box shows what the bank is really testing.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.34 tabulates the causes of acute visual loss and prints **CRVO, retinal detachment and vitreous haemorrhage in the same column as amaurosis fugax** — its \"Amaurosis Eugax [sic] (Transient ischaemic attacks)\" sits beside \"Central or branch retinal vein occlusion\", \"Retinal detachment\" and \"Vitreous hacmanhage [sic]\" under sudden painless loss. On the stem as printed, A, B and C are all defensible. The box reveals the discriminator the stem omits: **transience**. Amaurosis fugax is the only one of the four that goes away by itself in minutes. Keyed as printed; the ambiguity is recorded here and not disputed.\n\n**How the four differ once you know that.**\n\n| Option | Onset | Recovery without treatment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** CRVO | Sudden, over hours | No — the fundus keeps its haemorrhages for months |\n| **B** Retinal detachment | Sudden, often with a curtain and preceding flashes | No — it progresses |\n| **C** Vitreous haemorrhage | Sudden, with floaters | Slowly, over weeks as blood clears — not minutes |\n| **D** Amaurosis fugax | Sudden, painless, **monocular** | **Yes — minutes, complete** |\n\n**Where the module teaches it.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` names it once, of carotid embolic disease: \"May cause amaurosis fugax and occasionally permanent obstruction\". `ophthalmology.pdf` p.192 names it once more, inside the arteritic-AION Tutorial box — \"Amaurosis fugax may precede infarction of the optic nerve head\" — which is the same warning the bank's box gives. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss`, the module's own acute-loss deck, records in its register that transient visual loss and amaurosis fugax are **not named anywhere in it**; the term is taught in the book and in the vascular deck, not in the acute-loss lecture.\n\n**Why the box ends where it does.** The clinical weight of the term is not ocular. A transient monocular blindness is a retinal TIA, and the source of the embolus — carotid stenosis, or in an elderly patient giant cell arteritis — is what threatens the patient's life. That is the referral the box calls lifesaving.",
    objective:'Amaurosis fugax is transient monocular visual loss lasting minutes with full recovery — a retinal TIA that may precede CRAO and demands a search for its embolic source.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q605), key + explanation p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-606', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'What is the **visual field defect** shown?',
    options:['Right homonymous hemianopia','Right homonymous upper quadrantopia','Right homonymous lower quadrantopia','None of the above'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The figure is the whole question: two field circles side by side, each with its UPPER-RIGHT quadrant filled solid black and the other three quadrants clear.** Read it in three steps.\n\n1. **Both eyes are affected and the SAME side is black in each** → the defect is **homonymous**, so the lesion is behind the chiasm. A heteronymous defect (bitemporal, binasal) blacks out *opposite* halves; `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 15 draws that contrast.\n2. **Only a quarter of each field is gone, not a half** → **quadrantanopia**, not hemianopia.\n3. **The black quarter is upper and to the right** → a **right superior homonymous quadrantanopia**, from a lesion of the **left temporal lobe**.\n\n**Why the temporal lobe carries the upper field.** `L22` slide 20 draws Meyer's loop in two panels — \"As diagrammed…\" and \"…as seen from the side\" — with the looping fibres coloured **red** and the wedge of visual field they carry coloured red too: the **upper** wedge. The fibres that swing forward into the temporal lobe before turning back are the ones serving the superior field, so a temporal lesion takes the top quarter. Slide 21 then prints the two named defects side by side, both captioned *Contralateral homonymous quadrantanopia*: **\"Pie in the sky\"** (upper quadrant black, the temporal lesion) and **\"Pie in the floor\"** (lower quadrant black, the parietal lesion). `ophthalmology.pdf` p.195's numbered pathway figure says the same in a caption: \"4 Right superior homonymous quadrantanopia — **Lower fibres in temporal lobe**\", \"5 Right inferior homonymous quadrantanopia — Upper fibres in anterior parietal lobe\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — right homonymous hemianopia.** The whole right half of both circles would be black. That is the tract, geniculate or occipital picture (`L22` slides 17, 19, 23), not this one. Half is not a quarter.\n- **C — right homonymous lower quadrantopia.** Right side, right shape, **wrong quadrant** — the black would sit below the horizontal meridian. This is the *parietal* lesion, \"pie in the floor\".\n- **D — none of the above.** Only defensible if the figure were something else; B describes it exactly.\n\n**⚠️ Misprint kept in mind, not carried into the answer:** the bank prints option B as \"quadri**a**ntopia\" and option C as \"quadrantopia\" two lines later. The inconsistency inside one option list is what proves B a typographical slip rather than a variant spelling; the standard form is *quadrantanopia*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 itself prints \"quadrantopia\" in one figure caption and \"quadrantanopia\" in another.\n\n**What the picture cannot tell you.** Congruity. The two black quadrants would need to be compared for shape to say whether the lesion is anterior or posterior in the radiation, and `L22`'s own register records that congruity is drawn throughout the deck and defined nowhere.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Two fields with the upper-right quadrant lost is a right superior homonymous quadrantanopia — a left temporal lobe lesion taking Meyer’s loop, the "pie in the sky" defect.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q606), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-607', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'A **32-year-old woman** complains of **dull pain in her right eye** for the past one week **which worsens when moving her eye**. Her past medical history includes **multiple sclerosis** which was diagnosed 6 months ago. An ophthalmoscope shows **pallor of the optic disc**. Which anatomical site is most likely to be affected?',
    options:['Optic nerve','Optic radiation','Trigeminal nerve','Oculomotor nerve'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Pain on eye movement in a young woman with multiple sclerosis is optic neuritis until proved otherwise, and the book explains the pain anatomically.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 gives the mechanism in a parenthesis that is worth memorising whole: \"Periocular pain (**aggravated by eye movement due to traction of the extraocular muscles on inflamed meninges covering the optic nerve**)\". The optic nerve is the only cranial nerve wrapped in meninges, because it is a tract of the brain rather than a peripheral nerve — and the extraocular muscles take origin at the orbital apex right where those sheaths run. Inflame the sheath and every excursion of the globe tugs on it.\n\n**The other three findings all point the same way.** Age and sex fit — p.190's epidemiology is \"Most common in women ages 15 - 45\", and the Tutorial box on p.191 narrows the demyelinating form to \"Age 20-40 years old (mean around 30 years)\". The MS history fits — p.190 makes multiple sclerosis \"the most common cause\" of optic neuritis, and Q603 of this same topic keys exactly that. And the pale disc is the residue: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 files \"Demyelinating disease (MS): recurrent attacks\" as a cause of optic atrophy, and pallor \"in all types … due to decreased vascularity\".\n\n**Why the other three sites cannot produce this picture.**\n\n- **B — optic radiation.** Behind the geniculate body, and therefore behind the reach of optic atrophy: p.194 defines atrophy as degeneration \"from the retina to the lateral geniculate body\". A radiation lesion gives a homonymous field defect with a **normal disc** and no pain at all.\n- **C — trigeminal nerve.** The sensory nerve of the eye and orbit, so it *could* be blamed for pain — but pain alone. It has no role in vision and cannot make a disc pale, and trigeminal pain is not brought on by looking around.\n- **D — oculomotor nerve.** Its lesion is a motility problem: ptosis, a down-and-out eye, sometimes a dilated pupil (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182, `L30) Squint II` slides 11–13). Nothing in the stem is motor, and the third nerve does not carry vision.\n\n**A note on the timing.** The stem gives *one week* of pain and an *already pale* disc. Pallor after a single week is early — atrophy conventionally takes four to six weeks to become visible — which fits the stem's \"diagnosed 6 months ago\" and the box's \"recurrent attacks\": this is likely not her first episode. That reading of the interval is **not taken from the course material**; neither `L21) Optic Nerve` nor the book gives any time course for the development of pallor.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Pain worsened by eye movement comes from traction of the extraocular muscles on the inflamed meningeal sheath of the optic nerve — the localising symptom of optic neuritis in multiple sclerosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q607), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-608', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'For the **32-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis, a painful right eye and a pale optic disc** described in the previous question, the **treatment of choice** is?',
    options:['Panretinal photocoagulation','Pilocarpine eye drops','Corticosteroids','Peripheral iridectomy','Surgical extraction of lens'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Optic neuritis is an inflammatory demyelinating attack, and the only anti-inflammatory on the list is the answer. The other four options treat four different diseases, none of them this one.**\n\nThe regimen exists in the cache in exactly one place — the Tutorial box on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191, \"Demyelinating optic neuritis .. in more details (From Tutorial .. not in the book)\":\n\n> IV methyl prednisolone **1 g daily for 3 days**, followed by oral prednisolone (**1 mg/kg**) daily for **11 days**, then tapered over **3 days**.\n\nThat is the only drug regimen with doses anywhere in the whole neuro-ophthalmology chapter. `L21) Optic Nerve` names **no treatment of any kind** on any of its 20 slides — no drug, no dose, no route — so the lecture cannot supply this and the book's Tutorial box is the module's sole source. Note also the book's own marking: the contents page rules that Tutorial material is \"study in round & skip in final\", so the *doses* are examinable in the round rather than the paper; the *class* is core.\n\n**Why the other four belong to other diseases.**\n\n| Option | The disease it actually treats |\n|---|---|\n| **A** Panretinal photocoagulation | Proliferative **diabetic** retinopathy and other ischaemic retinopathies — it ablates ischaemic retina to remove the drive to new vessels |\n| **B** Pilocarpine eye drops | A miotic. It is used in **angle-closure glaucoma** to pull the peripheral iris out of the angle |\n| **D** Peripheral iridectomy | The definitive procedure for **pupil-block angle closure** — a hole in the iris that lets aqueous bypass the pupil |\n| **E** Surgical extraction of lens | **Cataract**. This patient's media are not mentioned, and her acuity loss is neural |\n\nNone of the four could act on an inflamed retrobulbar nerve, and two of them (B and D) would be actively wrong here — this eye is white, painful only on movement, and its pupil is defective for an **afferent** reason.\n\n**What steroid does and does not do.** The Tutorial box gives the regimen and no outcome claim. `ophthalmology.pdf`'s own register records that the Optic Neuritis Treatment Trial, the evidence base behind the intravenous route, is absent from the chapter, as is the rule that oral prednisone alone is inappropriate. Steroid is given to shorten the attack; the reader should not take from this key that it changes the final acuity or the rate of conversion to multiple sclerosis. That last qualification is **not taken from the course material** and is flagged so it is not mistaken for a printed claim.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Demyelinating optic neuritis is treated with corticosteroids — IV methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 3 days then oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg for 11 days, tapered over 3 days.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q608), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-609', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'All of the following is true about **papilledema** **except**:',
    options:['A raised optic nerve head.','Marked engorgement of the retinal veins.','Affects the vision early.','Bilaterally in most cases.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Papilledema is the disc swelling of raised intracranial pressure, and its most dangerous feature is that the vision stays good while the nerve is being strangled.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 states it twice. The definition: \"Swelling of the optic disc secondary to increased intracranial pressure, **It is typically bilateral**.\" And the N.B box: \"**Vision is not affected early in the condition** but transient visual obscuration or transient attacks of b[l]urred vision may be present.\" The chapter's Papilledema-vs-Papillitis table on p.189 puts it in the Symptoms row as \"Visual acuity usually **normal** … Transient attacks of blurred vision\" against papillitis's \"Marked visual deterioration\".\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8 — the lecturer's handwritten table — says the same in shorthand: **VA \"N\"** for papilledema against **\"↓\"** for papillitis, and Symptoms \"Transient obscuration (**sec**)\" against \"↓ VA\". The dashed Tutorial box on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 gives the whole course in three lines: \"Early: no visual symptoms · Later: transient visual loss for few seconds · **Latest: significant visual loss (optic atrophy)**\". Vision goes late, and when it goes it does not come back.\n\n**Why A, B and D are all correct findings.** The book's clinical picture of fully established papilledema on p.188 lists six signs, and three of them are the options:\n\n- **A — a raised optic nerve head.** Item 3, \"Blurring and **elevation** of the margins of the disc\". `L21` slide 8's last row grades it: \"Disc elevatn **+ + +**\" for papilledema against a single \"+\" for papillitis — the only quantitative comparison in the deck.\n- **B — marked engorgement of the retinal veins.** Item 2, \"**Tortuosity of the veins and capillaries** (venous congestion .. T)\". `L21` slide 6 photographs both eyes of one patient with grossly engorged, dark, dilated veins and flame haemorrhages at the disc margin.\n- **D — bilateral in most cases.** The definition's own qualifier, and the top row of the p.189 table. `L21` slide 8 adds the exception in brackets: \"Bilateral (**Except Foster Kennedy**)\", named once in the deck and never explained.\n\n**Cf. the topic's other two papilledema questions**, each with a different option menu: `opqb-t14-587` (which is NOT found — cupping) and `opqb-t14-591` (all characteristics EXCEPT — marked loss of vision). Read together they make one point three times: the disc is loud and the vision is quiet.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Papilledema is bilateral disc elevation with venous engorgement from raised intracranial pressure, and vision is characteristically spared early — only transient obscurations occur.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.95 (Q609), key p.100' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-610', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'In the **differentiation between papillitis and papilledema** the **most reliable indicator** is:',
    options:['The amount of elevation of the disc.','The degree of congestion.','The pupillary reaction.','The presence of vitreous opacities'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Papillitis\n\n- Unilateral (Acute onset)\n- Early vision affection\n  1. Decreased VA  2. Central VF defect\n  3. RAPD  4. Impaired color vision\n- Milder edema\n\nPapilledema\n\n- Bilateral\n- Vision is unaffected initially\n- No Central VF defect or Impaired color vision\n- More severe edema\n\n---\n\n**⚠️ The box printed under this key and the key itself point at DIFFERENT OPTIONS.** The box's only underlined-and-bolded line is \"**3. RAPD**\" — the pupillary reaction, which is **option C**. The printed key is **D, vitreous opacities**, and the box does not mention the vitreous at all. Keyed as printed above; both readings are set out here and neither is disputed.\n\n**The case for D, which the lecture makes and the book does not.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8 — the handwritten differential table, the densest slide in the deck — carries a row the book's table omits entirely:\n\n| Row | Papilledema | Papillitis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Vitreous** | **clear** | **Inflammatory cells** |\n\nThat row is genuinely absolute rather than relative. Cells in the vitreous mean inflammation, and raised intracranial pressure is not inflammatory, so the sign cannot appear in papilledema at any stage or severity. Slide 7's papillitis photograph is what that row looks like: the whole image is veiled and hazy, out of focus in a way no other fundus photograph in the deck is — and the deck never says the haze is the vitreous.\n\n**The case for C, which the box makes.** A relative afferent pupillary defect means one optic nerve is conducting worse than its fellow. Papilledema is bilateral and symmetrical, so it produces none; `L21` slide 8's RAPD row reads \"**No**\" for papilledema and **three tick marks** for papillitis, the only place the deck grades anything by emphasis. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.189's table gives Pupils: \"Normal\" against \"RAPD\".\n\n**Why A and B are the wrong kind of sign — and this is the point the question is really teaching.** Both are *quantitative* and both run the WRONG way from the intuition:\n\n- **A — amount of disc elevation.** `L21` slide 8 grades it \"+ + +\" for papilledema against \"+\" for papillitis; the book agrees, \"Disc swelling & elevation: More / Less\" and \"Disc oedema is milder than that of papilledema\" (p.189). So the *more* swollen disc is the *less* sight-threatening disease. It is a matter of degree with overlap, never a rule.\n- **B — degree of congestion.** This one **inverts**: the p.189 table gives \"Disc hyperemia: **Less** [papilledema] / **More** [papillitis]\", the opposite direction to the elevation row immediately above it. Two adjacent rows pointing opposite ways is precisely why neither can be relied on.\n\n**What to carry away regardless of which option you accept:** the reliable discriminators are the ones that are present-or-absent — laterality, RAPD, colour vision, a central scotoma, vitreous cells — and the unreliable ones are the ones measured in degrees.",
    objective:'Papillitis is separated from papilledema by all-or-none signs — unilaterality, RAPD, impaired colour vision, a central scotoma and vitreous cells — not by how swollen or how hyperaemic the disc looks.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q610), key + explanation p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-611', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy** commonly affects patients:',
    options:['In the first decade of life.','In the third decade.','In the seventh decade.','In the second decade'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**AION is a small-vessel disease of the elderly, and the seventh decade is the only option that lies inside any age band the book prints.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` gives three thresholds and they are all upward:\n\n| Where | What it says |\n|---|---|\n| p.191 | NAION \"can occur at any age but typically affects patients **over 40 years** of age who have atherosclerotic risk factors (e.g., hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidaemia, smoking, sleep apnoea, obesity)\" |\n| p.192 | \"All patients **more than 50 years** of age with suspected NAION should have an ESR and a CRP test to exclude giant cell (temporal) arteritis\" |\n| p.193 table | Non-arteritic: \"Majority **> 50** years old\" · Arteritic: \"Majority **> 60** years old\" |\n\nThe seventh decade is ages 60 to 69. It clears every one of those thresholds; the third decade (20–29) and the second (10–19) clear none, and the first (0–9) is not a vascular age at all.\n\n**Why the vessel makes it an old person's disease.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191 names the mechanism — \"transient **hypoperfusion of the posterior ciliary arteries** supplying the optic nerve head\" — and its arterial-supply figure separates that circulation from the central retinal artery, which is why AION damages the disc while sparing the retina. Posterior ciliary hypoperfusion needs an atherosclerotic bed and often a nocturnal dip in blood pressure: p.191 adds that \"Many occur during periods of **nocturnal hypotension**\", which is also why p.192's clinical picture opens \"Unilateral vision loss, **first noticed upon waking**\".\n\n**The lecture's picture of the same patient.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 9 has no text at all, but its first photograph is the temple and brow of an **elderly** person with grey hair and a thickened, tortuous, beaded artery standing out in relief against deeply wrinkled skin. ⚠️ The words *temporal arteritis* and *giant cell arteritis* are printed **nowhere in the deck** — the deck's own register records this — so the association has to be read off the photograph.\n\n**Why the distinction between the two forms matters at this age.** The arteritic form sits a decade higher (\"above 60 years\", p.192) and is the one that blinds the fellow eye \"within few days\" if untreated. That is the entire reason for the ESR/CRP rule above 50, and it is why an age answer here is not trivia.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy is a disease of atherosclerotic elderly patients — the book gives over 40, over 50 and over 60 as its bands, so the seventh decade is the fitting answer.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q611), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-612', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'A male patient **70-years-old** presented with **right sudden painless drop of vision**. Fundus examination showed **pale edematous disc**. Field of vision showed an **altitudinal field defect**. This picture is pathognomonic',
    options:['Papilledema.','Papillitis.','Retrobulbar neuritis.','Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Four elements, and every one of them is a printed line of the NAION picture. Together they exclude the other three diseases outright.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.192, clinical picture of NAION:\n\n> - **Unilateral** vision loss, first noticed upon waking.\n> - **Field loss of either the upper or the lower half** of the field of vision in the affected eye (**altitudinal field loss**).\n> - Decreased visual acuity\n> - Afferent pupillary defect\n> - Mild disc oedema **and pallor**.\n> - There are no other clinical manifestations or neurologic symptoms in cases of NAION.\n\n| Element of the stem | Where it comes from |\n|---|---|\n| Age 70 | p.193 table, \"Majority > 50 years old\"; p.191, \"typically … over 40\" |\n| Sudden and **painless** | p.193 table, Non-arteritic Local symptoms: \"Acute uniocular **painless** visual loss\" |\n| **Pale** oedematous disc | p.192, \"Mild disc oedema **and pallor**\" |\n| **Altitudinal** field defect | p.192, and `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 |\n\n**The pale swollen disc is the single most discriminating sign, and the lecture teaches it by contrast.** `L21` slide 5 prints three fundus photographs under one heading, two of them captioned. Photograph 1, *Papilloedema*: a **hyperaemic**, hot red-orange disc strewn with fluffy white-yellow exudate, margins completely obliterated, veins engorged and tortuous. Photograph 2, *AION*: a disc **swollen but pale**, a chalky whitish-pink, margins blurred but less angrily so, and **vessels of normal calibre, not engorged**. Putting them side by side is the point of the slide. A swollen disc that is pale rather than red is ischaemic, not congested.\n\n**The altitudinal defect is the second.** `L21` slide 4 — the lecturer's handwritten field page — writes \"**AION (ALtitudinal defect)**\" and sketches a broad hatched half-disc filling the LOWER half of the field with a deliberately flat horizontal upper border. Slide 9 then prints the deck's only real perimetry printout: the entire **superior** half solid black, stepping down to a straight edge along the horizontal meridian. A defect that respects the **horizontal** meridian is a nerve-head or retinal-vascular defect; only defects respecting the **vertical** meridian are chiasmal or post-chiasmal.\n\n**Why the other three fail.**\n\n- **A — papilledema.** \"It is typically **bilateral**\" and \"Vision is not affected early\" (p.188). This is unilateral with an immediate drop. Its field sign is an enlarged blind spot, not an altitudinal loss, and its disc is hyperaemic.\n- **B — papillitis.** The right laterality and the right acuity drop, but the wrong disc and the wrong field: p.189 gives papillitis \"**More**\" hyperaemia and a **central** scotoma, and `L21` slide 4 draws that central scotoma by hand under *Acute optic neuritis*. Papillitis is also a young person's disease — women 15 to 45 (p.190).\n- **C — retrobulbar neuritis.** Defined by a **normal disc**: p.190 tags it \"[normal optic disc]\" and Q643 of this topic keys \"May appear completely normal\". A pale oedematous disc excludes it by definition.\n\n**What the stem deliberately omits.** Any systemic symptom. p.193's table gives arteritic AION five systemic features — severe headache, temporal arteritis, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, proximal myalgia — and gives non-arteritic \"**No**\". Their absence, plus \"painless\", is what makes this the non-arteritic form. Even so, p.192 orders an ESR and CRP in every suspected NAION over 50.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A pale swollen disc with sudden painless monocular loss and an altitudinal field defect in an elderly patient is anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q612), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-613', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Optic nerve disorders** result in the following field defects **except**:',
    options:['Central scotoma.','Centrocecal scotoma.','Altitudinal field defect.','Nerve fiber bundle defect.','Homonymous hemianopia.'],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**A homonymous defect means the same side of space is lost in both eyes, and that is anatomically impossible in front of the chiasm — one optic nerve carries one eye.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 states the rule in one line: \"Optic nerve lesions: produce visual field defects **in the ipsilateral eye**; central scotoma or altitudinal hemianopia.\" It then reserves homonymous loss for everything behind the chiasm: \"Damage to the optic tract, the lateral geniculate body, the optic radiation, or the occipital cortex induces homonymous hemianopia.\" A single optic nerve has not yet met its fellow, so it cannot produce a matched defect in two eyes.\n\n**Options A to D are the book's own four, and the lecture's own five.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187's Tutorial box lists the field defects of optic nerve dysfunction:\n\n> - Central scotoma\n> - Altitudinal visual field defect (**AION**)\n> - Centrocaecal scotoma (**Tobacco-alcohol amblyopia**)\n> - Arcuate scotoma (**bundle nerve defect in glaucoma**)\n\nEach one has a disease attached, which is how it is meant to be learnt. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4 — the handwritten page — draws all of them plus one more, and the drawings are the teaching:\n\n| Disease | Defect | What slide 4 draws |\n|---|---|---|\n| Papilledema | enlarged blind spot | a small hatched circle off centre |\n| Acute optic neuritis | **central scotoma** | a hatched oval at the centre, blind spot beside it for scale |\n| Chronic RB neuritis | **centrocaecal scotoma** | a long oval **fused** to the blind spot — one continuous defect |\n| AION | **altitudinal defect** | a half-disc with a flat horizontal border |\n| Compression | sector / wedge (**bundle**) defect | a wedge widening outward from the blind spot |\n\nOption D's \"nerve fiber bundle defect\" is that last row and the book's arcuate scotoma: retinal ganglion cell axons run to the disc in arcs, so losing a bundle removes an arc-shaped strip of field. It is the classic glaucomatous defect and the compressive one.\n\n**⚠️ Misprint:** option E prints \"**Homonimus**\" for *homonymous*. Repaired in reading, kept in the transcription of what the page prints.\n\n**The rule to carry:** a defect that respects the **vertical** midline is chiasmal or post-chiasmal; a defect that respects the **horizontal** midline, or sits at fixation, or radiates from the blind spot, is an optic nerve or retinal defect.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Optic nerve lesions give monocular defects — central, centrocaecal, altitudinal or arcuate bundle scotomas; homonymous hemianopia requires a lesion at or behind the chiasm.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q613), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-614', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Bilateral** enlargement of the **blind spot** is present in:',
    options:['Consecutive optic atrophy.','Papilledema.','Open angle glaucoma.','Papillitis.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The word doing the work in this stem is \"bilateral\", and it is there to separate the two diseases that both enlarge the blind spot.**\n\nThe blind spot is the field's projection of the optic disc, which has no photoreceptors (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.9: \"because it **has no photoreceptors**, the area of the optic disc is called the 'blind spot'\"). Swell the disc and its footprint in the field grows. Both papilledema and papillitis swell the disc — so the enlargement is not the discriminator; the **laterality** is.\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.189's comparison table gives both rows:\n\n| Row | Papilledema | Papillitis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Laterality | **Bilateral** | Unilateral |\n| Field | **Enlarged blind spot** | Central scotoma |\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 8's handwritten table says the same, with the exceptions the lecturer adds in brackets: \"Bilateral (**Except Foster Kennedy**)\" against \"Unilateral (**Except NMO**)\", and Field \"enlarged Blindspot\" against \"central scotoma\". Slide 4's hand-drawn page pairs *Papilledema* with a small hatched circle drawn off centre — the enlarged blind spot itself — and pairs *Acute optic neuritis* with a hatched oval at fixation. Slide 6 prints both eyes of one papilledema patient side by side, which is the deck's way of stating bilaterality without words.\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 also has an uncaptioned figure showing the blind-spot projection of both eyes, labelled Pupil · Retina · Optic nerve — the anatomical basis of the sign.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — consecutive optic atrophy.** Atrophy from retinal disease. The disc **shrinks and pales**, it does not swell, so the blind spot does not enlarge. Its field loss comes from the retinal disease itself: `L21` slide 16 shows the picture with its cause visible — a waxy pale disc, thread-like vessels, and coarse bone-spicule pigment through the mid-periphery, i.e. retinitis pigmentosa, whose field loss is a ring and then a tunnel.\n- **C — open angle glaucoma.** The disc is **cupped**, not swollen; `L21` slide 10 shows the margin sharp and the cup excavated, and notes that it is the cup, not the margin, that is abnormal. Its field defect is the arcuate or nerve-fibre-bundle scotoma (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.187, \"Arcuate scotoma (bundle nerve defect in glaucoma)\"), which arcs *from* the blind spot rather than enlarging it.\n- **D — papillitis.** It does enlarge the blind spot, because the disc is swollen — but it is **unilateral**, so a *bilateral* enlargement is not its picture. This is the distractor the stem's first word exists to defeat.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A swollen disc enlarges the blind spot, and papilledema is the bilateral cause — papillitis does the same thing in one eye only.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q614), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-615', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'The **characters of the blind spot** are as follows, **except**:',
    options:['It is corresponding to the optic nerve head.','It lies temporal to the point of fixation.','It is described as absolute negative scotoma.','It is enlarged in cases with primary optic atrophy.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The blind spot enlarges when the disc SWELLS. Primary optic atrophy is the opposite state — a flat, pale, sharply bounded disc — so its blind spot is normal in size.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 defines primary optic atrophy as \"damage of the optic nerve occurring **outside of the eyeball**\" that \"occurs **without previous swelling** of the optic nerve head\". `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 14's first photograph, captioned *Primary*, shows what that means: a chalky white disc with **clearly defined, sharp margins**, a clean edge all round, normal-calibre vessels and a normal red-orange retina. Nothing about it is bigger than a normal disc. The disease that enlarges the blind spot is **papilledema** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 item 6, \"Visual field examination shows → enlargement of the blind spot\", and the Field row of the p.189 table — because the swollen disc's footprint in the field grows.\n\n**Why the other three are correct characters.**\n\n- **A — it corresponds to the optic nerve head.** By definition. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.9: \"In the visual field, and because it has no photoreceptors, the area of the optic disc is called the '**blind spot**'.\" The same identification is keyed twice elsewhere in this module's corpus, once in each bank — `opmcq-c1-41` keys *optic disc* and `opqb-t1-20` keys *blind spot* for the disc, the same fact asked from opposite directions.\n- **B — it lies temporal to the point of fixation.** The disc sits **nasal** to the fovea on the retina, and the retinal image is inverted left-to-right as well as up-and-down, so a nasal retinal structure projects into the **temporal** field. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.9's fundus figure shows the disc nasal to the macula, and p.188's blind-spot projection figure draws the two centres and the two blind spots of the pair. The optical inversion is the step the module leaves implicit.\n- **C — an absolute negative scotoma.** *Absolute* = nothing at all is seen within it, as against a *relative* scotoma where a dim or coloured target is missed but a bright one is seen. *Negative* = the patient is unaware of it, as against a *positive* scotoma which is perceived as a dark patch. Both are true of the physiological blind spot, which is why nobody notices theirs. ⚠️ This terminology is **not taken from the course material**: neither \"negative scotoma\" nor \"absolute scotoma\" appears anywhere in the 29 cached lecture files or the 12 cached book chapters (grepped case-insensitively, **zero hits for either phrase**). The book prints only the bare definition of *scotoma* — \"an area of reduced or lost retinal sensitivity surrounded by areas of normal retinal sensitivity\" (p.195) — and no absolute/relative or positive/negative axis.\n\n**A practical corollary.** Because the blind spot is negative, it is never a symptom; it is only ever a finding. A patient who *sees* a dark patch has a positive scotoma, and that is a different problem — retinal or macular rather than a disc landmark.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The blind spot is the optic disc projected temporal to fixation, an absolute negative scotoma; it enlarges with disc swelling (papilledema), not with primary optic atrophy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q615), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-616', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'A girl aged **20 years** came complaining of **rapid diminution of vision in one eye**. There was **pain on moving the eye up and in**. On examination an **afferent pupillary conduction defect** could be elicited. One of the following is likely to be the underlying cause:',
    options:['Hysterical.','Retrobulbar neuritis.','Acute congestive glaucoma.','Central retinal artery occlusion'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three findings, and all three are printed lines of the retrobulbar neuritis picture: a young woman, pain on eye movement, and an afferent pupillary defect.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 sets out the clinical picture of retrobulbar neuritis as symptoms and signs:\n\n> **Symptoms** — 1. Acute or subacute vision loss · 2. **Periocular pain (aggravated by eye movement due to traction of the extraocular muscles on inflamed meninges covering the optic nerve)**\n> **Signs** — 1. The optic disc may appear completely normal · 2. **Afferent pupillary defect** · 3. Decreased visual acuity · 4. Impaired colour vision · 5. Visual field defects.\n\nIts epidemiology on the same page — \"Most common in women ages **15 - 45**\" — is the stem's twenty-year-old girl.\n\n**Why \"up and in\" is the detail that matters.** The superior and medial recti take origin at the orbital apex from the annulus of Zinn, immediately around the optic nerve as it enters the canal, and they are the muscles that pull the eye up and in. Contracting them tugs hardest on the inflamed nerve sheath, which is why elevation and adduction are the movements classically named. The *mechanism* is the book's own sentence quoted above; the specific attribution of the worst pain to elevation and adduction, and the annulus anatomy behind it, is **not taken from the course material** — the book gives the traction mechanism without naming a direction.\n\n**⚠️ Pain on eye movement is absent from the lecture entirely.** `L21) Optic Nerve`'s own not-covered register, item 16, records it: \"Pain on eye movement — the classic discriminator for optic neuritis — is printed **nowhere in the deck**\", the deck's symptom teaching being the two cells \"Transient obscuration (sec)\" and \"↓ VA\" of the slide-8 table. The book is the only cached source for it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — hysterical (functional) visual loss.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.34 does list \"Hysterical (Functional) or Malingering\" among the causes of transient or sudden loss, so it is a real entry — but it is a diagnosis of exclusion and it cannot produce an **afferent pupillary defect**. The pupil reflex is not under voluntary control; an RAPD is objective evidence of organic optic nerve disease and settles this option immediately.\n- **C — acute congestive glaucoma.** Painful, yes, but the pain is severe, constant and not movement-related, and it comes with a red eye, a hazy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, haloes, nausea and a rock-hard globe. The stem gives none of that, and a mid-dilated fixed pupil is an efferent problem, not an afferent one.\n- **D — central retinal artery occlusion.** Sudden **painless** loss over seconds, with a pale oedematous retina, a cherry-red spot and thread-like arterioles. It does give a profound afferent defect, but it is a disease of the elderly and vasculopathic, and the pain on movement has no place in it. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` builds a whole case to that picture.\n\n**Cf. `opqb-t14-604`**, the post-influenza vignette, where the same syndrome is presented with a normal fundus and the question asks for the completing investigation rather than the diagnosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Rapid monocular visual loss with pain on eye movement and an afferent pupillary defect in a young woman is retrobulbar optic neuritis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q616), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-617', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'The following sign is characteristic of **consecutive optic atrophy**:',
    options:['A deep cup.','An underlying retinal disease.','A filled cup.','Kinking of the retinal vessels at the disc margin.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Consecutive means the atrophy is a consequence of something else, and that something is always in the retina — so the retina is where the diagnosis is made.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 gives the type two bullets and the second is the option:\n\n> **3. Consecutive optic atrophy:**\n> - Which occurs due to **retinal diseases** e.g., Retinitis pigmentosa / degenerative myopia (T)\n> - **Retinal examination shows the primary cause of the optic atrophy**\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` shows it twice. Slide 14's third photograph, captioned *Consecutive*, is a **waxy yellow** disc — distinctly yellow rather than white — with **markedly attenuated, thread-like arterioles** on a washed-out yellow-orange retina, quite unlike the healthy red behind the primary disc in the same block. Slide 16 then prints the same pattern with its cause in frame: the waxy pale disc at the left, grossly attenuated vessels, and **coarse black bone-spicule pigment clumps** through the whole mid-periphery — retinitis pigmentosa, though the slide names neither the pattern nor the disease.\n\n**Why the other three belong to the glaucomatous type.** All three are cup signs, and the cup is the glaucoma column:\n\n- **A — a deep cup.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194: glaucomatous atrophy is \"Caused by end stage chronic glaucoma (**advanced glaucomatous cupping**)\". `L21` slide 10 photographs it — a huge, brilliantly white, deeply excavated cup with only a thin crescent of rim surviving — and slide 11's four line drawings contrast a shallow normal excavation with a deep, undermined, U-shaped one.\n- **D — kinking of the retinal vessels at the disc margin.** The bayoneting of a cupped disc: `L21` slide 10 describes the trunk emerging nasally and the vessels bending **sharply over the steep cup wall**, disappearing behind its shoulder before reappearing on the floor. Slide 11's fourth drawing draws the vessels breaking at the cup margin. This is a glaucoma sign, not a consecutive one.\n- **C — a filled cup.** The opposite abnormality — a cup obliterated by swelling. It belongs to disc **oedema** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.187 lists \"Absence of a central cup (obliterated cup)\" among the ophthalmoscopic signs of optic disc swelling) and to the **secondary** atrophy that follows it, whose disc `L21` slide 14 shows as dirty grey-white with a cup \"filled in and not identifiable\".\n\n**The four types in one line each**, from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194: **primary** — damage behind the eye, no previous swelling; **secondary** — after long-standing papilledema or papillitis; **consecutive** — after retinal disease; **glaucomatous** — after end-stage chronic glaucoma. Cf. `opqb-t14-582`, which keys the causes of the primary type from the bank's own printed box.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Consecutive optic atrophy follows retinal disease, so the retina — attenuated vessels, pigmentary change — carries the diagnosis; the cup signs belong to glaucomatous atrophy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q617), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-618', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'**Optic nerve atrophy** can be **directly** due to the following **except**:',
    options:['Post glaucomatous.','Dendritic corneal ulcer.','Post optic neuritis or papilledema.','Ischemia due to giant cell arteritis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A dendritic ulcer is a disease of the corneal EPITHELIUM. It sits in front of the eye's optics, not on the visual pathway, so it can scar the cornea and blur the image without ever touching an axon of the optic nerve.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 defines optic atrophy as \"Degeneration of the optic nerve **at any part starting from the retina to the lateral geniculate body**\". The cornea is not on that segment — nor on any segment. Herpes simplex keratitis damages the epithelium and, if it recurs, the stroma; its visual loss is from corneal opacity, which is a **media** problem. The pupil reaction stays brisk and the disc stays pink, which is exactly the pair of findings that separates media disease from nerve disease at the bedside.\n\n**Why the other three do cause it, mapped onto the book's four types (p.194).**\n\n| Option | The type | The book's wording |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** Post glaucomatous | **Glaucomatous** | \"Caused by end stage chronic glaucoma (advanced glaucomatous cupping)\" |\n| **C** Post optic neuritis or papilledema | **Secondary** | \"Due to long-standing papilledema or papillitis\"; \"It is preceded by swelling of the optic nerve head\" |\n| **D** Ischaemia due to giant cell arteritis | Arteritic AION → optic atrophy | p.192, arteritic AION \"is caused by Giant cell arteritis (GCA)\"; p.193 gives it a \"Swollen and pale optic disc (**chalky white**)\" and VA \"Severely reduced (HM, PL)\" |\n\nOption C also carries the topic's most-discussed nuance: the book files **optic neuritis** under secondary only where it swelled the disc — its secondary column names \"papilledema or **papillitis**\" — while the retrobulbar form, which never swells, is filed under **primary**. That is the divergence recorded at `opqb-t14-584`.\n\n**⚠️ Giant cell arteritis is not in the lecture, and it is worth saying how that was measured.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 9, the AION slide, opens with a photograph of an elderly patient's temple showing a thickened, tortuous, beaded artery standing out in relief — and the deck's own not-covered register, item 4, records that \"**temporal arteritis**\" and \"**giant cell arteritis**\" are printed **nowhere on any of its 20 slides**. The picture is the whole of the lecture's teaching on it. The book carries the entity (p.192–193) and then delegates it onward: \"Please refer to the chapter on Ocular Manifestations of Systemic Conditions and Diseases\".\n\n**What GCA does that makes it the most urgent item on this list.** p.192's Tutorial box marks its management \"**URGENT !**\" — ESR \"very high (level of > 60 mm/hr)\", CRP highly elevated, temporal artery biopsy to confirm, and high-dose systemic steroid without waiting — because \"Other eye can become involved **within days**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Optic atrophy follows glaucoma, disc swelling and arteritic ischaemia; a dendritic corneal ulcer blurs vision by opacifying the media and never reaches the optic nerve.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.96 (Q618), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-619', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation** occurs in:',
    options:["Adie's pupil.","Horner's syndrome.",'Argyll Robertson pupil.','Pontine hemorrhage.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Argyll-Robertson pupils\n\n- Tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection\n- Bilateral (may be asymmetric)\n- **Small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation**\n- Investigations: serologic testing for syphilis and MRI of the brainstem\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.101. It is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.181 verbatim, bullet order included — and its third bullet is the stem word for word.)*\n\n**What light-near dissociation means.** The pupil does not constrict to light but does constrict when the eye fixes on something near. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 draws it as a two-panel figure — \"Pupils DO NOT constrict when exposed to bright light\" over \"Pupils DO constrict on a near object\" — and p.181 prints the mnemonic beside a cartoon: **ARP → Accommodation Reflex Present · PRA ← Pupillary Reflex Absent**. The lesion sits in the **pretectal area**, where the light-reflex fibres relay: `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 8 opens \"**Affection of pretectal area**\" and places the midbrain schematic beside it, then lists \"Small pupils · Light Near dissociation · Neurosyphilis · DM, Alcoholism, Encephalitis\". Its photograph shows both pupils markedly miotic and **irregular in outline**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **B — Horner's syndrome.** Small, yes — but **unilateral**, **round**, and with a **normal light reaction**. Horner is a loss of the sympathetic *dilator* supply, not of the light reflex; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 gives its four signs as miosis with anisocoria greater in the dark, ptosis with anhidrosis, enophthalmos and congenital heterochromia. Nothing there is dissociated and nothing there is irregular.\n- **D — pontine haemorrhage.** Small pupils, and famously so — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.179 files \"Pontine haemorrhage\" under **pin-point** pupil beside morphine and organophosphorus poisoning, and `L22` slide 3 lists it under Neurologic causes of miosis. But those pupils are round and the patient is comatose; there is no near reflex to dissociate in an unconscious person, and nothing makes them irregular.\n- **A — Adie's pupil.** The classic mimic, because it is the other cause of light-near dissociation — but its pupil is **large**, unilateral and tonic, and it is the exact opposite of small. ⚠️ **Adie's pupil is absent from every cached source in this module, and that was measured three ways**: `L22`'s own not-covered register, item 1, records that the deck \"names exactly three pupil syndromes — Marcus Gunn, Argyll Robertson and Horner, plus Dorsal Mid Brain Syndrome. **Adie appears on none of the 25 slides**\"; `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.14's register lists \"Adie's tonic pupil / Holmes-Adie syndrome — **not mentioned anywhere**\" as its first pupil gap; and ch.1's register lists it too. The description of it above is therefore **not taken from the course material**.\n- **The fourth cause the module does teach**, and the one worth pairing with this key: **dorsal midbrain syndrome**, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180's \"3 Ls\" — **L**ight-near dissociation, **L**oss of upgaze, **L**id retraction (Collier sign) — with hydrocephalus, midbrain compression, multiple sclerosis, stroke and midbrain haemorrhage as its causes and MRI mandatory. `L22` slide 9 gives it \"Pineal body tumors, Stroke, MS\" plus convergence-retraction nystagmus. Both it and Argyll-Robertson are pretectal, which is why both dissociate; the pupil **size and shape** is what tells them apart.\n\n**⚠️ Two misprints in the printed option list**, repaired above and recorded here: option A prints \"**Adies**\" for *Adie's*, option B prints \"**Homer's**\" for *Horner's*.",
    objective:'Small, irregular, bilateral pupils with light-near dissociation are Argyll-Robertson pupils — a pretectal lesion of tertiary syphilis, investigated by syphilis serology and brainstem MRI.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q619), key + explanation p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-620', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'In **complete third nerve paralysis** the **direction of the affected eye in the primary position** is:',
    options:['Inward.','Outward.','Outward and up.','Outward and down.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE BANK PRINTS THIS QUESTION TWICE IN THIS TOPIC AND KEYS IT TWO DIFFERENT WAYS.** `opqb-t14-594` on book p.94 has the identical stem and the identical four options — differing only in that this printing adds full stops — and it is keyed **D, \"Outward and down\"**. This printing, on book p.97, is keyed **B, \"Outward\"**. Both letters were read from enlarged crops of the two answer pages. Each entry is keyed exactly as its own page prints it and neither key is disputed; the contradiction is the bank's own.\n\n**What every cached source in the module says.** All of them say *down and out*, and they say it four times:\n\n| Source | Wording |\n|---|---|\n| `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182, prose | \"The eye is turned **outward and downward**\" |\n| `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182, figure call-out | \"**Down and out** — Unopposed left superior oblique and lateral rectus muscles\" |\n| `L30) Squint II` slide 11, bottom-right frame | the examiner's finger lifts the ptotic lid and the eye beneath is \"sitting **DOWN AND OUT**\" — iris carried toward the outer canthus and low in the fissure |\n| `L30` slide 12, lower photograph | the same manoeuvre in a second patient: deviated **out**, wide nasal scleral crescent, sitting **slightly low** |\n\n**Why down and out is the mechanically necessary answer.** The third nerve supplies four of the six extraocular muscles — medial rectus, superior rectus, inferior rectus and inferior oblique (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182's muscle map labels each). Paralyse all four and only two survive: the **lateral rectus** (CN VI), which abducts, and the **superior oblique** (CN IV), which the book's box on p.183 gives as \"Abduction / internal rotation / **depression**\". Two unopposed muscles, one pulling out and one pulling down — so the eye rests out and down. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.183's Tutorial table puts it another way: in complete third nerve palsy there is \"Limited ocular motility **except for abduction (lateral rectus) and slight depression (Superior oblique)**\".\n\n**Reading the options on that basis.** *Outward* (B) is half of the truth and *outward and down* (D) is the whole of it. *Inward* (A) is the sixth nerve's picture — `L30` slide 15 shows the esotropia of a sixth nerve palsy, the iris displaced nasally with a white crescent temporally. *Outward and up* (C) is wrong in its vertical component, since the surviving superior oblique depresses.\n\n**The examiner's caution this pair teaches, and it is the reason to read both entries together: a key letter is never the thing to memorise.** The same question keyed B on one page and D on another; what does not change is the anatomy — four muscles out, two muscles left, and the eye parks where those two pull it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Complete third nerve palsy leaves only the lateral rectus and the superior oblique working, so the eye rests abducted and depressed — "down and out" — beneath a ptotic lid.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q620), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-621', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A **45-year-old female** came complaining of **drooping of the both upper eye lids** with **double vision that increased in the evening**. The **best line of treatment** is:',
    options:['Lid surgery.','Prism glasses','Neostigmine injections.','Ocular muscle surgery'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The diagnosis is not in doubt: bilateral ptosis plus diplopia, both worse as the day goes on, is myasthenia gravis. The keyed treatment is.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.185's Tutorial box gives the ptosis of myasthenia in four lines and the stem reproduces three of them: \"Insidious, **bilateral** and frequently asymmetrical · **Worse at the end of the day** and least on awakening · Worse on prolonged upgaze due to fatigue\". The clinical picture on the same page opens \"**Ptosis and double vision are the presenting signs in half of the patients**\", and the box's summary of the disease is \"The most important feature is **fatigability** — muscle weakness worsens as the affected muscle is used and usually improves with rest\". `L30) Squint II` slide 18 photographs it without a word: three frames of the same man in sustained upgaze, the lids coming down further in each.\n\n**⚠️ KEY DIVERGENCE, recorded and not disputed.** The bank keys **B, prism glasses**. The treatment the book names for myasthenia is the drug class in **option C**: p.185's Tutorial box, under Treatment, reads \"There's no cure for myasthenia gravis, but Rx can help relieve signs & symptoms · May include **anticholinesterase drugs**, steroids, & immunosuppressive drugs\". Neostigmine is an anticholinesterase. Keyed as printed above.\n\n**How the keyed answer can be defended.** The book's *other* Tutorial box, the cranial-nerve-palsy intro on p.182, prints a general treatment scheme for any diplopia: \"Directed to eliminate the cause · Measures to eliminate diplopia — Monocular occlusion (temporary measurer) · **Prism (prescribed in glasses)** · Eye Alignment Surgery: for patients with long standing stable ocular misalignment\". A reader who treats the presenting *symptom* rather than the disease lands on prisms, which are the book's own second measure. It is also true that neither prisms nor drugs are unreasonable at a first visit, and that anticholinesterases are prescribed orally (pyridostigmine) rather than as the injections option C specifies — an injected anticholinesterase in this context is a **diagnostic** test, not a treatment. That last point is **not taken from the course material**: the book names the class without a route, and `L30` slide 19 captions the diagnostic injection only as \"**TensilonTest**\", a trade name, without naming edrophonium, a dose or a route.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture cannot arbitrate, and that was measured.** `L30`'s own not-covered register, item 20, records that \"**MYASTHENIA GRAVIS HAS NO TREATMENT SLIDE**. Pyridostigmine, steroids, immunosuppression, plasma exchange, IVIG and THYMECTOMY are named nowhere — the deck reaches the enlarged thymus on the chest film and stops.\" Item 21 adds that **no drug name or dose appears in the whole deck**. Its five myasthenia slides (17–21) cover the neuromuscular junction, the ptosis, the ice-pack and Tensilon tests, the \"any pattern\" motility failure and the investigations, and then end.\n\n**Why A and D are wrong on any reading.** Both are surgical, and surgery is contraindicated in a **variable** deviation. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 reserves eye alignment surgery \"for patients with **long standing stable** ocular misalignment\", and `L30` slide 16 gives the squint rule as after six months. A myasthenic lid and a myasthenic eye position change from hour to hour and improve with rest, so there is nothing stable to operate on — and operating on a ptosis that fluctuates risks exposure keratopathy on the good days.\n\n**Cf. `opqb-t14-635` and `opqb-t14-636`**, which take the same disease from its fatigability and its pupil.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Bilateral ptosis and diplopia worsening through the day is myasthenia gravis; the book treats the disease with anticholinesterases, steroids and immunosuppression, and the symptom with prisms — never with surgery on a variable deviation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q621), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-622', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A **diabetic man aged 55 years** presented with **diplopia**. The **diagnostic test** is:',
    options:['Refraction.','Intraocular pressure.','Ocular motility.','Visual acuity.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Diplopia in a vasculopath means a cranial nerve palsy until proved otherwise, and the test that finds a cranial nerve palsy is testing the eye movements.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182's Tutorial box gives the clinical presentation of any cranial nerve palsy in five lines, and three of them are motility findings: \"Manifest strabismus · **Limitation of movement in the direction of action of the paralyzed / paretic muscle** · Variability of the angle of deviation · **Binocular diplopia that increases in the field of action of affected muscle** · Abnormal head posture\". Motility testing is what converts a symptom into a localisation — it names the weak muscle, and the weak muscle names the nerve.\n\n**What diabetes makes likely, and what each palsy would look like on that test.**\n\n| Nerve | What motility testing shows | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **III** | ptosis, failure of adduction and elevation, the eye down and out | p.182; `L30) Squint II` slide 11 |\n| **IV** | hyperdeviation worst **in adduction**, lessening in abduction, with a head tilt | p.183; `L30` slide 14's nine-frame montage |\n| **VI** | esotropia in the primary position, abduction fails on that side | p.184; `L30` slide 15 |\n\nMicrovascular infarction of the nerve trunk is the shared mechanism: `ophthalmology.pdf` lists \"Microvascular infarction within the nerve as in **diabetes and hypertension**\" among the causes of the third (p.182), fourth (p.183) and sixth (p.184) nerve palsies alike, and for the sixth it is \"the most common cause in above **55** years of age individuals\" — the stem's exact age.\n\n**Why the other three tests answer nothing.**\n\n- **A — refraction.** Refractive error causes blur, and if it causes doubling at all the doubling is **monocular** and disappears when either eye is covered. A cranial nerve palsy gives **binocular** diplopia which vanishes when either eye is covered — that single manoeuvre separates the two, and it costs nothing.\n- **B — intraocular pressure.** Necessary in a diabetic eye examination in general, and neovascular glaucoma is a real diabetic complication, but a raised pressure does not cause double vision.\n- **D — visual acuity.** Recorded in every patient, and abnormal in many diabetics from retinopathy or maculopathy — but acuity is a measure of *resolution in each eye separately*, and diplopia is a failure of *alignment between* the eyes. A patient can be 6/6 in both eyes and see double.\n\n**One thing motility testing will not settle, and the stem's age is why it matters.** Whether the third nerve, if that is the one, is pupil-involving. That is a pupil examination, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182's figure makes it the branch point: pupil involved → trauma, aneurysm, tumour; pupil spared → vasculopathy. Cf. `opqb-t14-623` and `opqb-t14-624`, which are the same patient one question later.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Binocular diplopia is a problem of ocular alignment, so ocular motility testing is the diagnostic examination — it names the limited direction, the weak muscle and hence the palsied nerve.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q622), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-623', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A patient **60-year-old, diabetic for 20 years**. He suffered **acutely of drooping of the right eyelid**. **On manual elevation of the lid he sees double vision**. What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['Diabetic sixth nerve neuropathy.','Oculomotor nerve palsy.','Trochlear nerve palsy.','Abducent nerve palsy.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Ptosis is the discriminator, and only one cranial nerve on the list lifts the lid.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182's box on what CN III supplies puts the levator first: \"**Levator palpebrae superiosis** [sic] → ↑ lid · Sphincter pupillae → pupil constriction · The below EOMs\". Its clinical list opens with \"**Ptosis (masks the diplopia)**\" — which is precisely why the stem has to lift the lid before the patient can report double vision. That detail is not decoration; it is the sign, described from the inside. `L30) Squint II` slide 12 photographs the same manoeuvre: an elderly woman whose right-of-photograph lid has dropped to meet the lower so that only the lashes show, and beneath it a second frame in which the examiner's finger raises that lid to reveal an eye deviated out and sitting low.\n\n**Why the vignette fits a microvascular third nerve palsy exactly.** Diabetes of twenty years' standing, an **acute** onset, and a single nerve: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182 names \"Microvascular infarction within the nerve as in diabetes and hypertension (**the pupil is usually spared**) [external ophthalmoplegia only]\" as the second of its five aetiologies, after posterior communicating artery aneurysm.\n\n**Why the other three cannot produce ptosis.**\n\n- **A and D — \"diabetic sixth nerve neuropathy\" and \"abducent nerve palsy\".** ⚠️ These two options name **the same nerve twice**, which is itself the structure the question rests on: whichever way it is spelled, the sixth nerve supplies only the **lateral rectus** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.184's box, \"function of LR6: abduction\"). It has nothing to do with the lid. Its picture is an esotropia with failure of abduction and horizontal diplopia worse on gaze to the affected side — `L30` slide 15's montage, where the eye fails to reach the outer canthus on one side and both eyes move fully on the other.\n- **C — trochlear nerve palsy.** The fourth nerve supplies only the **superior oblique**. Its picture is a hypertropia worst in adduction, vertical diplopia worse in downgaze, and a compensatory head tilt away from the lesion (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.183; `L30` slide 14, where a child's head tilts and the higher eye rides up further still in adduction). No lid sign.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture supplies no cause for any of the three palsies, and that was measured.** `L30`'s own not-covered register, item 7, records that \"NO CAUSE IS GIVEN FOR ANY OF THE THREE NERVE PALSIES except the two on slide 13's diagram\" — a posterior communicating artery aneurysm and a vasa-vasorum infarct — and that \"**DIABETES**, HYPERTENSION, TRAUMA, TUMOUR, RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE… are named on no slide\". The diabetic attribution in this stem comes from the book alone.\n\n**The examination this vignette is still missing** is the pupil, which is what `opqb-t14-624` asks next.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Acute ptosis with diplopia revealed only when the lid is lifted localises to the third nerve, which alone supplies the levator; the fourth and sixth nerves carry no lid function.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q623), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-624', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'In the **60-year-old diabetic with an acute right ptosis and diplopia** described in the previous question, on **examination of the pupil** of this patient would you expect?',
    options:['Afferent pupillary conduction defect.','Reactive to accommodation not to light.','Dilated irreactive to light and accommodation.','Lost reaction to light and slow to accommodation.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ KEY DIVERGENCE, recorded and not disputed. The course material teaches that the pupil in a diabetic third nerve palsy is SPARED — and no option on this list offers a normal pupil.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` says it three times on two facing pages:\n\n| Where | What it says |\n|---|---|\n| p.182, aetiology | \"Microvascular infarction within the nerve as in diabetes and hypertension (**the pupil is usually spared**) [external ophthalmoplegia only]\" |\n| p.182, figure algorithm | \"Isolated cranial nerve III palsy → Pupil involved: Trauma · Aneurysm · Tumor — **Pupil spared: Vasculopathy**\" |\n| p.183, Tutorial table | Pupil-sparing 3rd nerve palsy: \"**Pupil: normal reaction**\" |\n\nThe mechanism is drawn on p.182 in the chapter's most exam-shaped figure: \"**Pupillary fibres lie dorsal and peripheral**\"; \"Blood vessels on pia mater supply surface of the nerve including pupillary fibres (damaged by compressive lesions)\"; \"Vasa nervorum supply part of nerve but not pupillary fibres (damaged by **medical lesions**)\". `L30) Squint II` slide 13 draws the same two-halved diagram and captions its two columns \"**Pupil involved (Compression)**\" and \"**Pupil spared (Vascular)**\", with a clinical photograph under each — the second showing both pupils of similar moderate size, that equality being the whole point of the column.\n\nSo on the book's own algorithm this patient's pupil should react normally. The bank keys **D**. Keyed as printed above; the discrepancy is recorded here and not disputed.\n\n**What the keyed option describes, and where it comes from.** \"Lost reaction to light and slow to accommodation\" is a *deep* parasympathetic lesion of the sphincter — the picture of a **pupil-involving** third nerve palsy, which p.183's Tutorial table gives as \"**Dilated non-reactive pupil and accommodation is lost** (Anisocoria more in light)\". The bank appears to be testing the complete-palsy row of that table rather than the vasculopathic one. If the vignette is read as a *complete* third nerve palsy — which the ptosis and diplopia allow — D is the row the book prints, and the word \"usually\" in p.182's parenthesis leaves the door open: pupil sparing in diabetes is a strong rule, not an absolute one.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong even on the bank's own reading.**\n\n- **A — afferent pupillary conduction defect.** An RAPD is a lesion of the **optic nerve**, the afferent limb, and it is diagnosed by the swinging-flashlight test (`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 6, *Marcus Gunn Pupil*). The third nerve is entirely **efferent**. A patient with an isolated third nerve palsy sees normally out of that eye and has no RAPD.\n- **B — reactive to accommodation not to light.** That is **light-near dissociation**, and it belongs to the pretectum — Argyll-Robertson (`opqb-t14-619`) and dorsal midbrain syndrome. It requires an intact efferent third nerve to mediate the near response, so it cannot coexist with a paralysed one.\n- **C — dilated, irreactive to light and accommodation.** Closest to the truth for a complete palsy, and this is the option the reader is most likely to mark. It is p.183's own wording almost exactly. The only thing separating it from D is D's \"**slow** to accommodation\" against C's total loss.\n\n**The clinical point that survives whichever option is marked.** In an isolated third nerve palsy the pupil decides the urgency: a **spared** pupil in a diabetic is a nerve infarct that recovers, while an **involved** pupil is a posterior communicating artery aneurysm until imaging says otherwise — p.183's investigation line is \"MRI and cerebrovascular imaging to exclude compression from posterior communicating artery aneurysms\", and p.182 notes the aneurysmal palsy \"is usually painful\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The pupillary fibres run on the outside of the third nerve, so compression takes them and a diabetic infarct usually spares them — the pupil is what separates an aneurysm from a nerve infarct.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q624), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-625', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'The **most common cause of sixth cranial nerve palsy** is:',
    options:['Diabetic neuropathy.','Vascular occlusion.','Idiopathic.','Trauma.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The book never names an overall commonest cause. It gives two, each qualified by an age band, and the bank keys the paediatric one without its qualifier.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.184, aetiology of sixth nerve palsy, in the order printed:\n\n> - Compressive lesions (e.g., intracranial tumour) in **20%** of cases (especially in adults younger than 45 years of age)\n> - Microvascular infarction within the nerve as in diabetes and hypertension (**the most common cause in above 55 years of age individuals**) — spontaneous recovery usually occurs within 3-4 months\n> - Increased intracranial pressure as in idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri) or hydrocephalus. This can lead to bilateral VI palsy\n> - **Trauma (most common cause in children)**\n> - Other causes in children: inflammation of the petrous part of the temporal bone in severe otitis media · post viral infections\n\nSo the two \"most common\" claims in the chapter are **microvascular infarction above 55** and **trauma in children**. The stem asks the question unqualified and the bank keys **trauma**. Recorded here; the key stands as printed.\n\n**Why the sixth nerve is the one that gets injured and stretched.** It has the longest intracranial course of the ocular motor nerves and climbs the clivus over a sharp bony edge to enter the cavernous sinus, so head injury and any rise in intracranial pressure both catch it. That anatomical explanation is **not taken from the course material** — the book states the associations and never gives the reason. What the book does add is the consequence: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 prints a highlighted box, \"**N.B: Sixth nerve affection can also occur due to increased intracranial pressure (A false localizing sign)**\", and `opqb-t13-523` of this corpus keys lateral rectus palsy as the way pseudotumor cerebri affects the eye.\n\n**How the other three options map onto the book's list.**\n\n- **A — diabetic neuropathy** and **B — vascular occlusion** are two names for the *same* entry, microvascular infarction of the nerve, which the book makes commonest **above 55**. Offering it twice splits the vote of anyone reasoning from the adult population, which is presumably why both appear.\n- **C — idiopathic.** The book's list has no idiopathic entry for the sixth nerve at all; it uses the word only inside \"**idiopathic** intracranial hypertension\", which is a named mechanism rather than an absence of one.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture is silent on causes, measured.** `L30) Squint II` slide 15 shows a sixth nerve palsy as a diagram plus a nine-frame montage — esotropia in the primary position, full adduction, failed abduction — and names no cause. Its own not-covered register, item 7, records that \"DIABETES, HYPERTENSION, TRAUMA, TUMOUR, RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE… are named on no slide. **The sixth nerve's classic false-localising role in raised ICP is absent.**\" Everything in this entry beyond the pictures comes from the book.\n\n**One prognostic figure worth keeping** from the same page: a microvascular sixth nerve palsy recovers spontaneously, usually within **3–4 months**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The book gives two age-qualified answers for sixth nerve palsy — trauma in children, microvascular infarction above 55 — and adds that raised intracranial pressure makes it a false localising sign.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q625), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-626', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**After neck surgery** patient complained of **enophthalmos** at the side of surgery. On examination there was **miosis** on the same side. The cause may be:',
    options:['Atrophy of retro bulbar fat.','Paralysis of Muller muscle.','Paralysis of inferior tarsal plate muscle.',"Paralysis of both Muller's muscle and inferior tarsal plate muscle."],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"This is classic for Horner syndrome, which results from interruption of the sympathetic pathway.\n\n- **Ptosis** → due to paralysis of Muller's muscle (upper lid)\n- **Inverse ptosis / elevation of lower lid** → due to paralysis of the inferior tarsal muscle\n- **Miosis** → due to loss of sympathetic supply to the dilator pupillae\n- **Apparent enophthalmos** → caused by the combined upper and lower lid ptosis\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.101. Its last line is the answer: the enophthalmos is **apparent**, and it takes **both** lids to produce it — which is why the key is D and not B or C alone.)*\n\n**The globe has not moved.** Both lids are pulled toward the palpebral fissure by small sympathetically innervated smooth muscles — Müller's muscle in the upper lid, which adds roughly 2 mm of elevation above the levator's action, and the inferior tarsal muscle in the lower lid, which holds it down. Lose the sympathetic supply and the upper lid falls while the lower rides **up**: the fissure narrows from both directions and the eye *looks* sunken. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180's sympathetic-pathway figure maps each sign onto its lost branch — \"Nerve to dilator muscle (✗ Miosis) · Nerve to tarsal muscle / **Muller muscle: keeps eyelid elevated** (✗ ptosis) · Nerve to sweat gland (✗ Anhydrosis)\".\n\n**Why the neck surgery is in the stem.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 lists \"**Neck, thoracic, or spinal trauma / surgery**\" among the causes of Horner syndrome. The sympathetic supply to the eye takes a three-neurone detour through the chest and up the carotid — `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 10 numbers the six stations: ① hypothalamus, ② T1, ③ superior cervical ganglion, ④ carotid artery, ⑤ cavernous sinus to the nasociliary nerve, ⑥ dilator pupillae, lacrimal glands, **Müller's muscles**, orbital vessels. Anything done to the neck can interrupt it at stage ③ or ④.\n\n**⚠️ The four causes the book flags in red**, because they are why a new Horner syndrome is an emergency and not a curiosity: apical lung (Pancoast) tumour · thoracic aortic aneurysm · carotid artery dissection · cavernous carotid aneurysm. p.180 prints \"[life-threatening]\" beside exactly those four and no others. Its investigations are an MRI \"from the base of the brain to the mid-thoracic region\" and carotid angiography in acute or painful cases — the imaging range following the pathway's own length.\n\n**Why A, B and C fail.**\n\n- **A — atrophy of retrobulbar fat.** This produces a **true** enophthalmos, and it is a real cause of one (senile fat atrophy, wasting illness, and the enophthalmos that follows an orbital floor fracture as contents prolapse). But it is not caused by neck surgery, it is not acute, and it cannot explain the miosis. The miosis is what forces a sympathetic answer.\n- **B — paralysis of Müller's muscle** alone. Half the mechanism. It gives the ptosis but not the elevated lower lid, and the box explicitly attributes the apparent enophthalmos to the **combination**.\n- **C — paralysis of the inferior tarsal muscle** alone. The other half, and the one usually forgotten. Its sign has its own name in the box — **inverse ptosis**.\n\n**Cf. `opqb-t14-598`**, the *EXCEPT* question on Horner's signs, which turns on the fissure **narrowing** rather than widening, and `opqb-t14-619`, which separates Horner's round unilateral miosis from the small irregular bilateral pupils of Argyll-Robertson.\n\n**⚠️ The option list as printed** gives D as \"Paralysis of both **Muller'** muscle and inferior tarsal plate muscle\" — a stray apostrophe with no *s*. Repaired above.",
    objective:'The enophthalmos of Horner syndrome is apparent, not real: sympathetic denervation drops the upper lid (Müller’s muscle) and raises the lower (inferior tarsal muscle), narrowing the fissure from both sides.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.97 (Q626), key + explanation p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-627', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-va',
    stem:'**Amsler grid** is a good screening test for:',
    options:['Whole visual field.','Central field.','Peripheral field.','Contraction of the field.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The Amsler grid is a small square of graph paper held at reading distance, so the only part of the field it can sample is the part immediately around fixation.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.23 defines it in one sentence: \"**Amsler grid**: it is used to check for field defects in the **central 10 degrees** (central field) for each eye at a time.\" `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 123 prints a single line for it in its field-testing tree — \"Amsler Grid → **For the central 10° of the visual field**\" — and the deck's own register records that this is the whole of it: \"'Amsler' = **1 hit in the deck**\", with no technique and no interpretation printed.\n\n**Why ten degrees.** The chart is 10 cm square with 5 mm squares and is held at 30 cm; the geometry puts each half-side at about 10 degrees of arc from fixation. Distortion of the lines (metamorphopsia) or a missing patch (a central scotoma) is what the patient reports. That geometry and the metamorphopsia usage are **not taken from the course material** — both sources give the 10-degree figure and stop.\n\n**Why the other three cannot be tested this way.**\n\n- **A — the whole visual field.** The normal field extends roughly 90 degrees temporally, 60 nasally and superiorly, 70 inferiorly. A card held in the hands covers a fraction of that. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.23 gives the tests that do reach the whole field: **confrontation**, which needs an acuity of 1/60 or better with the examiner at 1 m, and **automated perimetry**, \"an accurate quantitative test that can be used for assessment and follow-up\".\n- **C — the peripheral field.** The grid contains no peripheral points at all, so a patient with total peripheral loss and an intact macula reads it perfectly. This is why glaucoma cannot be screened with it.\n- **D — contraction of the field.** A concentric narrowing from the periphery inward, which by definition happens outside the ten degrees. Detecting it needs perimetry or confrontation.\n\n**Where this fact recurs in the corpus.** `opqb-t1-41` (same bank, topic 1) asks \"The Amsler grid assesses:\" and keys \"Central 10° field\" from a different option menu; `opmcq-c1-14` (House) asks it from the opposite direction. All three are the same one-line fact from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.23, asked three ways.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The Amsler grid screens only the central 10 degrees of field, one eye at a time; the whole field and the periphery need confrontation or automated perimetry.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q627), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-628', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'A female patient complains of **infertility, galactorrhea, headache and defective vision**. CT scan showed **large sellar tumor**. Field examination may show:',
    options:['Binasal hemianopia.','Bitemporal hemianopia','Right congruous homonymous hemianopia.','Left incongruous homonymous hemianopia.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A sellar tumour presses on the chiasm from below and from the midline, and a midline chiasmal lesion takes the crossing nasal fibres of both eyes — bitemporal hemianopia.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 names the causes in one line: \"Optic chiasm lesions: Give rise to visual field defects that affect both eyes. **Bitemporal hemianopia caused by pituitary adenoma, craniopharyngioma, or parasellar meningioma**.\" The stem's triad — infertility, galactorrhoea and a sellar mass in a woman — is a **prolactinoma**, the commonest pituitary adenoma, and it is the first name on that list. (The endocrine syndrome itself is **not taken from the course material**: prolactin, galactorrhoea and amenorrhoea appear nowhere in the ophthalmology decks or the cached book chapters; the stem supplies them and the ophthalmology is what the question tests.)\n\n**Why the temporal halves.** The temporal half of each eye's field falls on the **nasal** retina, and nasal fibres are the ones that decussate. A mass growing up out of the sella meets the chiasm at its middle, where those crossing bundles lie, and spares the uncrossed temporal fibres running along its lateral edges. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 15 draws it and captions it *Bitemporal Hemianopia*: two circles, the left one black on its LEFT half, the right one black on its RIGHT half — **opposite** halves, which is what *heteronymous* means. Its picture 3 marks the chiasm with a cyan bar, and picture 2 is a coronal section through the pituitary fossa showing the pituitary in orange with the chiasm sitting directly above it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — binasal hemianopia.** The mirror-image rarity, and the option's whole purpose is to catch a reader who has learnt \"chiasm\" without learning \"which part of the chiasm\". It needs **bilateral lateral** compression, taking the uncrossed temporal fibres on both edges at once. `L22` slide 16 is the deck's only slide on it, and its figure — the only field pair in the deck that is actually labelled \"LE\" and \"RE\" — shows the inner half of each field lost, with red masses drawn pressing on the chiasm from **both sides**.\n- **C and D — homonymous hemianopias.** Both require a lesion **behind** the chiasm, where each side of the pathway already carries the opposite half of space from both eyes. A sellar tumour is in front of and below that point. As a bonus, the two options label themselves out of contention: a *congruous* defect points to the occipital cortex (`L22` slide 23) and an *incongruous* one to the tract or geniculate body (slides 17 and 19) — three sites, none of them the sella.\n\n**The clinical corollary.** Because the loss starts in the upper temporal quadrants and creeps down, patients do not notice it early — there is no blur at fixation and the two eyes' remaining nasal fields overlap. The headache and the endocrine symptoms usually arrive first, which is why the field examination in this stem is confirmatory rather than diagnostic. That inference is **not taken from the course material**; the book states the defect and its causes and gives no natural history for it.\n\n**⚠️ THREE MISPRINTS in one option list**, repaired above and recorded here: option B prints \"**Bitermporal**\", option C prints \"con**gronous**\" for *congruous*, option D prints \"in**congronous**\" for *incongruous*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A pituitary adenoma compresses the chiasm in the midline and takes the crossing nasal fibres of both eyes, producing a bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q628), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-629', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'An **area of reduced or absent vision within an otherwise intact visual field** is called:',
    options:['Scotoma','Homonymous hemianopia.','Heteronymous hemianopia.','Altitudinal hemianopia.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The stem is the book's own definition with two words changed.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 opens its field-defect section with a terminology table, and the first row reads:\n\n> **Scotoma** — an area of reduced or lost retinal sensitivity **surrounded by areas of normal retinal sensitivity**\n\nThe defining feature is the *surround*: a scotoma is an island of loss inside a sea of seeing field. That is what separates it from every other option, all three of which are **hemianopias** — losses of a whole half of the field that reach the edge and respect a meridian.\n\n**The rest of the table, since the other three options are its other rows:**\n\n| Term | The book's definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Hemianopia** | \"Loss of half the visual field. Usually, this involves loss of either the right or the left half of the visual field in **both eyes**\" |\n| **Altitudinal hemianopia** | \"Loss of the **superior or inferior** half of the visual field\" |\n| **Homonymous hemianopia** | \"Loss of either the right or the left half of the visual field in **both eyes**\" |\n| **Bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopia** | \"Loss of the **temporal** half of the visual field in both eyes\" |\n\nSo the three distractors are not random: **B** is loss of the *same* side in both eyes (post-chiasmal), **C** is loss of *opposite* sides in the two eyes (chiasmal), and **D** is loss of the top or bottom half of one eye's field (optic nerve or retinal vascular). None of them is surrounded by normal field.\n\n**The scotomas this module actually names**, all of them optic nerve defects, from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187's Tutorial box and `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4's handwritten page:\n\n- **Central scotoma** — at fixation; acute optic neuritis.\n- **Centrocaecal scotoma** — fixation and blind spot fused into one defect; chronic retrobulbar neuritis, tobacco-alcohol amblyopia.\n- **Arcuate (nerve fibre bundle) scotoma** — an arc sweeping from the blind spot; glaucoma, compression.\n- **The enlarged blind spot** — papilledema's version of the same idea.\n\n**One thing the module does not print**, and it is the axis that makes the term clinically useful: the absolute-versus-relative and positive-versus-negative distinctions. Neither phrase appears in any of the 29 cached lecture files or the 12 cached book chapters (grepped case-insensitively, **zero hits**). See `opqb-t14-615`, where the bank asks about them anyway.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A scotoma is an area of reduced or lost retinal sensitivity surrounded by normal field — an island of loss, unlike a hemianopia, which takes a whole half and respects a meridian.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q629), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-630', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Central scotoma** may be due to:',
    options:['An ipsilateral optic nerve lesion.','Chiasmal lesion.','Optic tract lesion.','Cortical lesion'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A central scotoma is a monocular defect, and the only monocular segment of the pathway is the optic nerve.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 prints the rule as its first bullet on lesion sites: \"**Optic nerve lesions:** produce visual field defects **in the ipsilateral eye** — **central scotoma** or altitudinal hemianopia — accompanying afferent pupillary defect.\" Everything from the chiasm backwards involves both eyes, because from the chiasm onward each side of the pathway carries fibres from both retinas.\n\n**Why the macular fibres are the ones that go first.** The papillomacular bundle — the axons serving the fovea — enters the temporal side of the disc as a compact, heavily myelinated bundle, and it is selectively vulnerable in inflammatory, toxic and nutritional optic neuropathy. That anatomical explanation is **not taken from the course material**: neither `L21) Optic Nerve` nor `ophthalmology.pdf` names the papillomacular bundle. What both do give is the **disease-to-defect mapping**, and it is the thing to learn:\n\n| Disease | Defect | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Acute optic neuritis / papillitis | **central scotoma** | `L21` slide 4; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.189 table, Field row |\n| Chronic retrobulbar neuritis (B12, tobacco-alcohol, drugs) | **centrocaecal** scotoma | `L21` slide 4; p.187, p.190 |\n| AION | altitudinal defect | `L21` slides 4 and 9; p.192 |\n| Glaucoma / compression | arcuate or wedge defect | p.187; `L21` slide 4 |\n| Papilledema | enlarged blind spot | p.188; `L21` slide 8 |\n\n`L21` slide 4's sketch for acute optic neuritis is a hatched oval sitting at the centre of the field with a smaller hatched circle beside it — the blind spot, drawn at normal size for scale. Sitting at fixation is what makes a central scotoma so destructive to acuity while leaving the patient able to walk about.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **B — chiasmal lesion.** Affects both eyes and gives a **heteronymous** defect respecting the vertical midline, bitemporal or (rarely) binasal — `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slides 15 and 16.\n- **C — optic tract lesion.** Both eyes, **homonymous**, and **incongruous** — `L22` slide 17's field pair, its two black halves drawn deliberately unequal.\n- **D — cortical lesion.** Both eyes, homonymous, congruous, typically with macular **sparing** — `L22` slide 23, whose figure cuts a small white notch out of the centre of each black half. That is almost the exact opposite of a central scotoma: a cortical lesion is the one post-chiasmal site that characteristically *keeps* the centre.\n\n**The corroborating sign the book attaches to the answer**, and the reason a central scotoma is worth chasing: an optic nerve lesion comes with an **afferent pupillary defect**. A macular lesion can also produce a central scotoma, but not an RAPD of any size — which is how the two are told apart at the bedside.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A central scotoma is a monocular defect of the ipsilateral optic nerve, accompanied by an afferent pupillary defect; chiasmal and post-chiasmal lesions affect both eyes.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q630), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-631', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Altitudinal hemianopia** may be due to',
    options:['Posterior cerebral cortical lesion.','Chiasmal lesion.','Optic tract lesion.','Branch central retinal artery occlusion.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**An altitudinal defect respects the HORIZONTAL meridian, and the horizontal meridian is a retinal and optic-nerve-head boundary, not a neurological one.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195 defines the term — \"**Altitudinal hemianopia**: Loss of the **superior or inferior half** of the visual field\" — and files it with the optic nerve: \"Optic nerve lesions … central scotoma or **altitudinal hemianopia**.\" Every defect behind the chiasm respects the **vertical** midline instead, because that is where the two halves of space are divided between the hemispheres.\n\n**Why the retinal circulation draws a horizontal line.** The central retinal artery divides at the disc into a **superior** and an **inferior** branch, and each supplies its own half of the retina with almost no crossing at the horizontal raphe. Occlude one branch and a horizontal half of retina dies, taking the opposite half of that eye's field. The same watershed governs the optic nerve head's posterior ciliary supply, which is why AION — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.192, \"**Field loss of either the upper or the lower half** of the field of vision in the affected eye (altitudinal field loss)\" — draws the identical line. The anatomical detail of the horizontal raphe is **not taken from the course material**; the book gives the defect and its diseases without the vascular anatomy behind it.\n\n**What the lecture shows.** `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4's handwritten page writes \"**AION (ALtitudinal defect)**\" and sketches a broad hatched half-disc filling the lower half of the field with a deliberately flat horizontal upper border. Slide 9 then prints the deck's only real perimetry printout: the **entire superior half** solid black, stepping down to a straight edge along the horizontal meridian, with the inferior half normal.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — posterior cerebral cortical lesion.** Homonymous, congruous, macula-sparing, respecting the vertical midline (`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 23).\n- **B — chiasmal lesion.** Bitemporal or binasal — again vertical (`L22` slides 15 and 16).\n- **C — optic tract lesion.** Homonymous and incongruous — vertical (`L22` slide 17).\n\nAll three are neurological and none of them can produce a horizontal cut-off. The option list is really a single test: *does this defect respect the vertical meridian or the horizontal one?*\n\n**Where this sits in the corpus.** `opqb-t13-540` asks the retinal half of the same idea from the other side — which conditions do **not** give a sectorial or altitudinal defect — and lists branch retinal artery occlusion among those that do. Cf. `opqb-t14-612` and `opqb-t14-639`, which key the altitudinal defect to AION.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An altitudinal defect respects the horizontal meridian, so it comes from a branch retinal artery occlusion or AION — never from a chiasmal, tract or cortical lesion, which all respect the vertical.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q631), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-632', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'Which is **more decisive test for optic nerve dysfunction**?',
    options:['Visual acuity.','Afferent pupillary conduction defect.','Confrontation test.','Fluorescein angiography.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A relative afferent pupillary defect is objective, it cannot be feigned or blurred away, and it compares one nerve directly against the other. That is what makes it decisive.**\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 3 is the deck's only typed bullet list and it prints the answer as a piece of typography. Its five manifestations of optic nerve dysfunction are each given an icon, and the icons carry the meaning:\n\n| Icon | Item |\n|---|---|\n| ↓ large red down arrow | Visual acuity |\n| ↓ large red down arrow | Colour vision |\n| ↓ large red down arrow | Contrast sensitivity |\n| **★ red star** | **Field defect** |\n| **★ red star** | **RAPD** |\n\nThe first three are marked as things that are merely *reduced*; the last two are **starred** — the lecturer's way of flagging the two signs that localise the lesion to the nerve. Option B is one of the two starred items and option A is one of the three arrowed ones.\n\n**Why acuity is the weaker test.** It is subjective, it is a single number for a single eye with nothing to compare against, and it is lowered by anything in the light path — a refractive error, a cataract, corneal disease, a macular problem. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187's Tutorial box lists it first among the signs of optic nerve dysfunction, but an eye can have a substantial optic neuropathy at 6/6 and an eye with 6/60 may have nothing wrong with its nerve at all.\n\n**Why the RAPD is different in kind.** It is a **reflex**, so the patient cannot influence it, and it is **relative**, so it is measured as a difference between the two eyes rather than as an absolute. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 6 photographs the test in four frames — the *Marcus Gunn Pupil* — a torch swung from eye to eye: shining on the good eye constricts both pupils, and swinging to the bad eye lets both **dilate** again. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187 draws the same as \"Normal\" versus \"Right relative afferent pupillary defect\", three rows of eye pairs labelled *Constricts* and *Dilates*. It appears in the papilledema-versus-papillitis table (p.189, \"Pupils: Normal / RAPD\") and in the sign list of retrobulbar neuritis, optic neuritis and NAION alike.\n\n**Why the other two are not tests of the nerve at all.**\n\n- **C — confrontation.** A field test, so it is testing the *other* starred item — but crudely. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.23 gives its constraints: it needs an acuity of **1/60 or better** and the examiner sits at **1 m**, comparing the patient's field against his own. It will find a hemianopia and miss a small central scotoma. The accurate version is automated perimetry, which is not on the list.\n- **D — fluorescein angiography.** An imaging test of the **retinal and choroidal circulation**, not of neural function. It shows leakage, blockage and non-perfusion; it cannot measure whether axons are conducting.\n\n**⚠️ Misprint:** option D prints \"**Fluorescien**\" for *Fluorescein*. Repaired above.\n\n**One limitation worth knowing.** A **symmetrical bilateral** optic neuropathy produces no RAPD, because the test is a comparison — if both nerves are equally damaged there is no relative difference to find. That caveat is **not taken from the course material**; `L21`'s own register records that RAPD is listed twice in the deck and never explained, the abbreviation never expanded and the swinging-flashlight test never named.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The relative afferent pupillary defect is the decisive sign of optic nerve dysfunction — objective, unfeignable and comparative — while acuity is subjective and lowered by anything in the light path.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q632), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-633', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A child **six years old** presented with **bilateral congenital dense cataract dating since birth**. What would you expect him to have?',
    options:['Unilateral concomitant convergent squint.','Bilateral optic atrophy.','Ocular nystagmus.','Normal ocular motility'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Both eyes have been deprived of a formed image since birth, so fixation never developed — and an eye that never learns to fixate wanders.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.208 prints the rule as a two-branch consequence of untreated congenital cataract:\n\n> If treated late or left untreated:\n> - **unilateral** congenital cataract can lead to **sensory squint**\n> - **bilateral** congenital cataract can lead to **nystagmus** with poor visual prognosis for both conditions\n\nThat is this question and its pair, `opqb-t14-634`, in two lines. The laterality in the stem chooses the branch: **bilateral** → nystagmus.\n\n**Why bilateral deprivation gives nystagmus and unilateral gives squint.** Steady fixation is learnt during the first months of life and it needs a clear retinal image to be learnt from. Deprive **one** eye and the other still learns to fixate, so the system stays steady and the deprived eye simply drifts — a sensory squint. Deprive **both** and neither eye ever acquires the fixation reflex, so the eyes oscillate: sensory or deprivation nystagmus. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.205 lists \"**Form deprivation and occlusion amblyopia** (Opacities in ocular media such as cataract, corneal scarring, or upper eyelid ptosis\" as a predisposing factor for amblyopia, and p.208's Tutorial box adds that \"Often, pediatric cataracts result in some degree of **amblyopia**\". The developmental mechanism above is **not taken from the course material** — the book states the two outcomes without explaining why laterality decides which.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — unilateral concomitant convergent squint.** The right *kind* of answer for the wrong laterality: it is what the book gives for a **unilateral** congenital cataract, not a bilateral one. Nothing here would make one eye deviate rather than the other.\n- **B — bilateral optic atrophy.** A cataract is a **media** opacity in front of the retina; it degrades the image without damaging an axon. Optic atrophy is \"degeneration of the optic nerve at any part starting from the retina to the lateral geniculate body\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.194) and a lens opacity is not on that segment. Cf. `opqb-t14-618`, where a corneal ulcer is excluded from the causes of optic atrophy on exactly this reasoning.\n- **D — normal ocular motility.** The point of the six-year interval in the stem: the critical period for visual development has long closed. This is the answer to `opqb-t14-634`, where the opacity appeared at seven or eight and lasted six months.\n\n**⚠️ Sensory nystagmus is not named in any cached ophthalmology deck.** `L30) Squint II` slide 22 is the module's whole nystagmus content and its four bullets are \"End Gaze · Congenital · Drug induced · Aquired [sic]: Vestibular, Cerebellar, Brain stem\" — no deprivation category. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.186's table of the three benign forms gives congenital nystagmus \"found at birth or in the first 6 months of life\" with \"subnormal vision\", which is as close as the module comes. The book chapter that carries the fact is the paediatric one, p.208.\n\n**⚠️ Note also that this is not a neuro-ophthalmology question by topic** — it is a paediatric cataract question printed inside the bank's neuro-ophthalmology section, as is its pair.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Untreated bilateral congenital cataract leads to nystagmus because fixation never develops; the unilateral form leads instead to a sensory squint.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q633), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-634', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A child **eight-year-old** presented with **traumatic right dense cataract of six-month duration**. What would you expect him to have?',
    options:['Right concomitant convergent squint.','Ocular nystagmus.','Limited eye movements.','Normal ocular motility.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The contrast with the previous question is not the density of the opacity — both are dense — it is WHEN it appeared. This child had seven and a half years of normal binocular vision before the injury.**\n\nPut the pair side by side:\n\n| | `opqb-t14-633` | This question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opacity | bilateral, dense | right, dense |\n| Present since | **birth** | age 7½ (six months ago) |\n| Fixation reflex | never developed | **fully developed** |\n| Expected | **nystagmus** | **normal ocular motility** |\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.208 attaches its two adverse outcomes specifically to **congenital** cataract — \"If treated late or left untreated: unilateral congenital cataract can lead to sensory squint · bilateral congenital cataract can lead to nystagmus\" — and to the failure to treat it early: \"Visually significant cataract requires **immediate** treatment once diagnosed to avoid **amblyopia**.\" The urgency exists because the visual system is plastic only for a limited period after birth. An opacity arriving at seven and a half years arrives after that window has shut, so the motor consequences do not follow. The formulation of the critical period is **not taken from the course material**; the book conveys it through the word *immediate* and through the congenital/acquired split rather than by naming a duration.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — right concomitant convergent squint.** This is the book's outcome for a **unilateral congenital** cataract — a **sensory** squint, in an eye that never learnt to fixate. A well-established fusion reflex holds the eyes straight even when one image is lost, so an acquired monocular opacity at this age does not deviate the eye. (An adult who loses vision in one eye may develop a sensory exotropia over years; that is a slow, late change and it is **not taken from the course material**.)\n- **B — ocular nystagmus.** Needs **bilateral** deprivation from birth, so that neither eye ever acquires steady fixation. One eye here still sees perfectly.\n- **C — limited eye movements.** A motility restriction implies a mechanical or neurological problem — a muscle entrapped in an orbital floor fracture, or a cranial nerve palsy. A cataract is inside the eye and cannot restrict the globe's excursion. If the trauma that caused the cataract also fractured the orbit, that is a separate injury and the stem does not describe one.\n\n**What this child WILL have, which the option list does not offer.** Loss of stereopsis and of binocular single vision while the eye is opaque, and a real risk of **amblyopia** if it is left untreated — p.208's Tutorial box notes that paediatric cataracts \"often result in some degree of amblyopia\", and p.205 lists media opacity as a predisposing factor for deprivation amblyopia. The eight-year-old sits at the very edge of treatable amblyopia, which is why the six-month duration in the stem is a worrying number rather than a reassuring one. The treatment is unchanged: p.208, \"surgical removal of the cataractous lens\", with visual rehabilitation by glasses, bifocals or contact lenses **and eye-patching**.\n\n**⚠️ Like its pair, this is a paediatric cataract question printed inside the bank's neuro-ophthalmology topic.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A dense cataract acquired at eight years old comes after the fixation reflex is established, so ocular motility stays normal — the squint and nystagmus of the congenital forms do not follow.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q634), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-635', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A patient complains of **fatigability of muscle function on sustained effort**. Positive family history is present. What do you expect **the most common presenting sign** of the attack?',
    options:['Failure of respiration.','Inability to climb the stairs.','Inability to carry heavy objects.','Ptosis and double vision.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Myasthenia gravis usually announces itself in the eye, and the book gives a figure for how often.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.185's clinical picture of myasthenia gravis opens with the answer: \"**Ptosis and double vision are the presenting signs in half of the patients**.\" Its Tutorial box on the same page repeats it from the other direction: \"In more than half of patient with myasthenia gravis, their first signs and symptoms involve **eye problems**, such as: ptosis and diplopia.\" The stem's opening clause is the box's own definition of the disease — \"The most important feature is **fatigability** — muscle weakness worsens as the affected muscle is used and usually improves with rest, so muscle weakness can come and go.\"\n\n**Why the eye muscles go first.** They are small, they work continuously, and they have a low safety factor at the neuromuscular junction, so a partial block of acetylcholine receptors shows there before it shows anywhere else — and a millimetre of levator weakness is visible, while the same proportional weakness in a limb is not. `L30) Squint II` slide 17 draws the junction itself in two panels: normal transmission on the left, and on the right the receptors capped by Y-shaped antibodies with the label \"**Receptors blocked by antibody**\" and \"**Reduced transmission**\". The low-safety-factor explanation is **not taken from the course material**; the book and the deck give the frequency without the reason.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and they are not wrong because they are impossible.** All three occur in myasthenia — they are simply not what patients present with. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.185's Tutorial box lists them under \"Other manifestation may include\":\n\n| Option | Where the book puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **A** Failure of respiration | \"Difficulty with breathing is **rare but serious**\" — the fifth of six items |\n| **B** Inability to climb the stairs | \"Weakness, particularly of the arms & **proximal leg** muscles\" — the sixth and last |\n| **C** Inability to carry heavy objects | the arm half of that same last item |\n\nSo the option list is ordered against the book's own list: A is explicitly rare, B and C are the final item, and D is the first line of the clinical picture with a fifty-per-cent figure attached.\n\n**What `L30` shows for the keyed answer.** Slide 18 photographs the ptosis twice — an elderly man with both lids drooping heavily and his brows lifted and forehead furrowed from frontalis over-action, and beside it a three-frame sequence of a younger man in sustained upgaze in which the lids come further down in each successive frame. The word *fatigue* is never printed; the three frames are the demonstration. Slide 20 handles the diplopia with two red words, \"**Any pattern**\", over thirteen photographs in which the limitation changes from frame to frame instead of following one muscle.\n\n**⚠️ On the stem's \"positive family history\".** The book gives myasthenia no familial claim at all: p.185 calls it \"A chronic **autoimmune** condition that interferes with the neuromuscular transmission in skeletal muscles\" and its epidemiology is \"it can affect any age or gender\", with the Tutorial box adding \"more common in women < 40 and in men > 60\". Autoimmune disease clusters in families in general, and there are separate congenital myasthenic syndromes that are genuinely inherited, but neither point is printed anywhere in the module — both are **not taken from the course material**. The family history is best read as scene-setting rather than as a diagnostic criterion; the fatigability is what makes the diagnosis.\n\n**The follow-on question, `opqb-t14-636`, asks about the pupil in this same patient.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ptosis and diplopia are the presenting signs in half of all myasthenia gravis patients; limb weakness and respiratory failure are later or rare manifestations.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.98 (Q635), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-636', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'In the **patient complaining of fatigability of muscle function on sustained effort, with a positive family history**, described in the previous question, which statement is correct regarding **the pupil**?',
    options:['The pupil may be dilated in severe cases','The pupil may react slowly to light','The pupil is never affected','The pupil shows light-near dissociation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Clinical picture of MG:\n\n- Ptosis and double vision are the presenting signs in half of the patients\n- May simulate any other ocular motor paresis/ paralysis (third, fourth, or sixth nerves)\n- **THE PUPIL IS NEVER AFFECTED IN MG.**\n- Weakness of other skeletal muscles in the body leading to fatigue with sustained effort.\n- patients with unexplained ptosis or ocular movement problems, myasthenia gravis suspected.\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.101. It is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.185 verbatim, capitals and all — the book prints that third line in capitals and highlights it, which is unusual enough in this chapter to be worth noticing.)*\n\n**Why \"never\" is safe to say here, when medicine rarely allows the word.** Myasthenia is a disorder of the **nicotinic** acetylcholine receptor at the skeletal neuromuscular junction. The iris sphincter and the ciliary muscle are **smooth** muscle driven by **muscarinic** receptors, and the antibodies do not bind them. `L30) Squint II` slide 17 draws the mechanism: a normal terminal with acetylcholine crossing to blue receptors, beside a myasthenic one whose receptors are capped by Y-shaped antibodies, labelled \"Receptors blocked by antibody · Reduced transmission\". The nicotinic-versus-muscarinic distinction is **not taken from the course material** — neither source names the receptor subtype; the book asserts the rule and the deck draws the antibody.\n\n**Why the rule is diagnostically valuable, and it is the whole reason the book capitalises it.** The box's second line says myasthenia \"May **simulate any other ocular motor paresis/paralysis** (third, fourth, or sixth nerves)\", and `L30` slide 20 makes the same point in two red words — \"**Any pattern**\" — over thirteen photographs in which the limitation changes from frame to frame instead of following one muscle. So a myasthenic can look exactly like a third nerve palsy: ptosis, a deviated eye, limited elevation and adduction. **The pupil is what breaks the tie.** A third nerve palsy may involve the pupil; myasthenia never does. Put the other way round, a ptosis with a **dilated** pupil is not myasthenia, and that patient needs imaging for an aneurysm (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182–183).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and where each belongs.**\n\n- **A — dilated in severe cases.** The pupil-involving third nerve palsy: p.183's Tutorial table, \"Dilated non-reactive pupil and accommodation is lost (Anisocoria more in light)\". Severity of myasthenia does not recruit the pupil, because the receptor is the wrong one at any severity.\n- **B — reacts slowly to light.** A partial efferent lesion, or a tonic pupil. It is the shape of `opqb-t14-624`'s keyed option.\n- **D — light-near dissociation.** Pretectal: Argyll-Robertson (`opqb-t14-619`) and dorsal midbrain syndrome, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180's \"3 Ls\". Nowhere near the neuromuscular junction.\n\n**What the module does test for instead.** `L30` slides 19 and 21: the **ice-pack test** (the book adds the timing, an ice pack on the lid for **2 min**, \"as cold improves neuromuscular transmission\", and notes it is negative in non-myasthenic ptosis), the **Tensilon test** — the deck captions it as one word, \"TensilonTest\", and never names edrophonium or a dose — and then **acetylcholine receptor antibodies**, **EMG**, and **chest CT or X-ray for an enlarged thymus**, which slide 21 shows in a PA and a lateral film.",
    objective:'The pupil is never affected in myasthenia gravis, which is what separates a myasthenic ptosis with limited motility from a third nerve palsy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q636), key + explanation p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-637', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Pupillary nerve fibers** pass through the **optic tract** to:',
    options:['The optic chiasma','The optic radiation.','The lateral geniculate body.','The pretectal nuclei.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The pupillary fibres travel with the visual fibres as far as the back of the optic tract and then leave it, peeling off before the lateral geniculate body to reach the pretectum. That branch point is the anatomy this question tests.**\n\n`L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 5 is the module's diagram of the light reflex, and its labels run top to bottom exactly in the order of the arc: **Superior colliculus · Pretectal nucleus · Lateral geniculate body · Edinger-Westphal nucleus · 3rd Nerve · Ciliary ganglion · Sphincter pupillae**. The afferent limb is drawn in blue and the efferent in red, and the two nuclei at the top — pretectal and Edinger-Westphal — are drawn as **paired yellow circles either side of the midline with their connecting fibres decussating between them**. That decussation is the point of the figure and it is drawn rather than written: it is why light in one eye constricts **both** pupils.\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.179's numbered pathway figure spells the same four steps out in words:\n\n> 1. Light is shined only on the left eye.\n> 2. Action potentials from the left eye reach **both right and left pretectal nuclei**\n> 3. The pretectal nuclei stimulate **both sides** of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus.\n> 4. Edinger-Westphal nuclei generate action potentials through the right and left oculomotor nerves, causing **constriction of both pupils**.\n\n`L22`'s slide 8 corroborates the localisation from the pathological side: Argyll-Robertson pupils are headed \"**Affection of pretectal area**\", and their signature is a lost light reflex with a preserved near response — exactly what a lesion at the pretectal relay, downstream of vision and upstream of the near pathway, would produce.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — the optic chiasma.** In front of the tract, not behind it. The stem says the fibres pass **through** the tract, so the destination must be posterior to it.\n- **B — the optic radiation.** Two relays too far. The radiation begins **after** the lateral geniculate body and carries conscious vision to the occipital cortex; `L22` slide 13 labels the whole chain — Retina · Optic Nerve · Optic Chiasm · Optic Tract · Lateral Geniculate Nucleus · Optic Radiation · Primary Visual Cortex. The pupil pathway never joins it, which is why a cortically blind patient still has reactive pupils.\n- **C — the lateral geniculate body.** The most tempting distractor, because it is the tract's actual terminus for **visual** fibres. The pupillary fibres are the ones that do **not** synapse there — they leave just short of it. `L22` slides 18 and 19 devote two whole slides to the geniculate body's six layers and its field defect and say nothing about the pupil.\n\n**⚠️ Misprint:** option C prints \"lateral **genicualte** body\". Repaired above.\n\n**The corollary worth carrying.** Because the reflex has a two-eye afferent and a two-eye efferent, a lesion of one **optic nerve** gives a *relative* afferent defect (both pupils under-react when that eye is lit — `L22` slide 6's Marcus Gunn pupil) while a lesion of one **third nerve** gives an anisocoria that is worse in the light. Afferent problems are symmetrical between the pupils; efferent problems are not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Pupillary afferent fibres run with the visual fibres to the back of the optic tract, then leave before the lateral geniculate body to synapse in the pretectal nuclei, which project bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q637), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-638', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'**Ocular motility testing** is important to detect all the following **except**:',
    options:['Strabismus.','Nystagmus.','Limitation of Gaze.','Ciliary muscle paralysis.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Motility testing watches the eye move through space. The ciliary muscle does not move the eye at all — it moves the lens, inside the eye.**\n\nThe ciliary muscle is an **intraocular** smooth muscle. Contracting it slackens the zonules and lets the lens become more convex, which is accommodation; paralysing it (cycloplegia) leaves the patient unable to focus at near while the eye still turns perfectly in every direction. It is tested by **near vision** — the near point of accommodation, an amplitude in dioptres — not by asking the patient to follow a target. Nothing an examiner can see in the nine positions of gaze would reveal it.\n\n**What motility testing does detect, option by option.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182's Tutorial box lists the clinical presentation of a cranial nerve palsy and three of its five lines are motility findings — \"**Manifest strabismus** · **Limitation of movement in the direction of action of the paralyzed / paretic muscle** · Variability of the angle of deviation · Binocular diplopia that increases in the field of action of affected muscle · Abnormal head posture\".\n\n| Option | How motility testing shows it |\n|---|---|\n| **A** Strabismus | the eyes are misaligned in the primary position — `L30) Squint II` slide 15's esotropia, slide 11's exotropia beneath a ptotic lid |\n| **B** Nystagmus | involuntary rhythmic oscillation seen while the eyes hold, or move to, a position. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.186 defines **end-gaze** nystagmus as occurring \"when the patient is attempting to maintain the eyes in extremes of lateral gaze\", so it is only found by taking the eyes there |\n| **C** Limitation of gaze | the whole purpose of the nine-position montage — `L30` slides 11, 14 and 15 are each a montage showing which eye fails to reach which position |\n\n`L30) Squint II` slide 3 is the deck's own \"Ocular Motility\" slide: the nine positions of gaze as a montage with a direction diagram. Every one of A, B and C is read off that grid; D is not on it.\n\n**Note that both muscles of the option list belong to the same nerve.** The third nerve supplies four extraocular muscles *and*, through its parasympathetic fibres and the ciliary ganglion, the sphincter pupillae and the ciliary muscle — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.182's box lists \"Levator palpebrae superiosis → ↑ lid · Sphincter pupillae → pupil constriction · The below EOMs\". A complete third nerve palsy therefore paralyses accommodation too: p.183's Tutorial table gives complete palsy \"Dilated non-reactive pupil **and accommodation is lost**\". So ciliary paralysis is a real finding in the same disease — it is simply not found by this test. Testing near vision or the pupil is what finds it.\n\n**The general rule:** motility testing examines **extraocular** muscle function. Intraocular muscle function — the sphincter, the dilator, the ciliary muscle — is examined by the **pupil and accommodation** examination.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ocular motility testing examines the extraocular muscles and finds strabismus, nystagmus and gaze limitation; the ciliary muscle is intraocular and its paralysis shows only on testing accommodation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q638), key p.101' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-639', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'The **most characteristic visual field defect in NAION** is:',
    options:['Central scotoma','Altitudinal field defect','Bitemporal hemianopia','Constricted peripheral field'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Clinical picture of NAION\n\n- Unilateral vision loss, first noticed upon waking.\n- **Field toss** of either the upper or the lower half of the field of vision in the affected eye (altitudinal field loss).\n- Decreased visual acuity\n- Afferent pupillary defect\n- Mild disc oedema and pallor.\n- No other clinical manifestations or neurologic symptoms in cases of NAION.\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.102 — the answer page the contents page never mentions. ⚠️ \"**Field toss**\" is the bank's own typo: this box is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.192 copied line for line and the book prints \"**Field loss**\". Kept verbatim above, as the bank set it.)*\n\n**Why an altitudinal defect and not any other shape.** The optic nerve head is supplied by the **posterior ciliary arteries**, and that supply is divided into an upper and a lower territory with a watershed running horizontally across the disc. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.191 names the mechanism — NAION \"is caused by transient **hypoperfusion of the posterior ciliary arteries** supplying the optic nerve head\" — and its arterial-supply figure separates that circulation from the central retinal artery, which is why the disc infarcts while the retina looks normal. Infarct half of that territory and half of the nerve head dies, taking the corresponding horizontal half of the field. The watershed anatomy behind it is **not taken from the course material**; the book gives the vessel and the defect without the segmental supply that links them.\n\n**What the lecture shows.** `L21) Optic Nerve` gives it twice. Slide 4, the lecturer's handwritten page, writes \"**AION (ALtitudinal defect)**\" and sketches a broad hatched half-disc filling the **lower** half of the field with a deliberately flat horizontal upper border. Slide 9 prints the deck's only real perimetry printout, and it goes the other way: the entire **superior** half solid black, stepping down to a straight edge along the horizontal meridian, with a band of mid-grey just below the edge and a normal inferior half. Either half can go — which is exactly what the box's \"either the upper or the lower\" means.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — central scotoma.** The optic **neuritis** defect: `L21` slide 4 pairs it with *Acute optic neuritis*, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.189's table gives papillitis \"Central scotoma\" in its Field row. This is the single most useful contrast in the topic, because NAION and optic neuritis are the two acute optic neuropathies. Cf. `opqb-t14-640`.\n- **C — bitemporal hemianopia.** A **chiasmal** defect affecting both eyes and respecting the **vertical** midline (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.195; `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 15). NAION is monocular.\n- **D — constricted peripheral field.** A concentric narrowing, belonging to advanced glaucoma, retinitis pigmentosa and functional visual loss. It respects no meridian at all.\n\n**The rule underneath the answer:** a defect respecting the **horizontal** meridian is a disc or retinal-vascular defect; a defect respecting the **vertical** meridian is chiasmal or post-chiasmal. Cf. `opqb-t14-631`, which tests the same rule from the retinal side.",
    objective:'NAION infarcts one horizontal half of the optic nerve head’s posterior ciliary territory, so its field defect is altitudinal — upper or lower half loss respecting the horizontal meridian.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q639), key + explanation p.102' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-640', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'Which clinical feature **differentiates NAION from optic neuritis**?',
    options:['Decreased visual acuity','Pain with eye movement','Afferent pupillary defect','Visual field defect'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three of the four options are printed in BOTH diseases' clinical pictures. Only one is printed in exactly one of them.**\n\nSet the book's two lists side by side. Optic neuritis, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190–191; NAION, p.192:\n\n| Feature | Optic neuritis | NAION |\n|---|---|---|\n| Decreased visual acuity | ✔ signs list, item 3 | ✔ clinical picture |\n| Afferent pupillary defect | ✔ signs list, item 2 | ✔ clinical picture |\n| Visual field defect | ✔ signs list, item 5 | ✔ altitudinal field loss |\n| **Pain with eye movement** | ✔ \"**Periocular pain (aggravated by eye movement…)**\" | ✘ — p.193's table gives non-arteritic AION \"Acute uniocular **painless** visual loss\" |\n\nSo A, C and D are shared and cannot discriminate; **B is present in one and explicitly denied in the other**.\n\n**Why optic neuritis hurts on movement.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 gives the mechanism in the parenthesis that is the single most quotable line in the section: pain \"aggravated by eye movement **due to traction of the extraocular muscles on inflamed meninges covering the optic nerve**\". The optic nerve is a tract of brain and carries meningeal sheaths; the recti take origin at the orbital apex beside them. Inflame the sheath and every excursion tugs on it.\n\n**Why NAION does not.** It is an infarct of the nerve head, not an inflammation of the sheath. p.192 goes further and closes the door on every other symptom too: \"There are **no other clinical manifestations or neurologic symptoms** in cases of NAION.\"\n\n**The other separators the option list does not offer**, all of them stronger in practice than any single symptom:\n\n| | Optic neuritis | NAION |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Age** | women 15–45; mean around 30 (p.190, p.191) | majority > 50 (p.193); typically over 40 (p.191) |\n| **Disc** | normal in retrobulbar; hyperaemic and swollen in papillitis | **swollen and pale** (p.192, p.193) |\n| **Field** | usually **central** scotoma (p.191) | **altitudinal** (p.192) |\n| **Recovery** | usually recovers | \"Little or no visual recovery is expected\" (p.192) |\n| **Onset** | subacute over days | on waking, from nocturnal hypotension (p.191, p.192) |\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 5 draws the disc row as two photographs under one heading: *Papilloedema*, a hyperaemic hot red-orange disc strewn with white-yellow exudate and engorged tortuous vessels, beside *AION*, a disc swollen but **pale**, chalky whitish-pink, with vessels of normal calibre. That contrast — congested versus ischaemic — is the deck's whole teaching on the difference.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture cannot supply the keyed feature, and that was measured.** `L21`'s own not-covered register, item 16, records that \"**Pain on eye movement** — the classic discriminator for optic neuritis — is printed **nowhere in the deck**\". This key rests on the book alone.\n\n**One caution the arteritic form imposes.** *Arteritic* AION does hurt — p.193's table gives it \"Sudden severe unilateral visual loss **+ periocular pain**\", and p.192 adds temporal pain, scalp tenderness and jaw claudication. So \"painless\" separates **non-arteritic** AION from optic neuritis, which is what the stem specifies.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Reduced acuity, an afferent pupillary defect and a field defect occur in both; pain worsened by eye movement occurs in optic neuritis and not in non-arteritic AION, which is painless.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q640), key p.102' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-641', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'Which of the following patients is **most likely to present with retrobulbar optic neuritis**?',
    options:['60-year-old man with sudden painless vision loss','25-year-old woman with decreased visual acuity and periocular pain','70-year-old diabetic with altitudinal field defect','35-year-old man with chronic glaucoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Each option is a different disease compressed into a one-line vignette, and only one of them matches the book's epidemiology and its two cardinal symptoms at the same time.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 gives optic neuritis the epidemiology \"Most common in **women ages 15 - 45**\", and the Tutorial box on p.191 narrows the demyelinating form further: \"Common in **female** · Age **20-40** years old (mean around 30 years)\". A twenty-five-year-old woman sits in the middle of both bands. Her two symptoms are the book's own two, in order:\n\n> **Symptoms** — 1. Acute or subacute vision loss · 2. **Periocular pain** (aggravated by eye movement due to traction of the extraocular muscles on inflamed meninges covering the optic nerve)\n\nSo option B is not merely the least wrong; it reproduces the printed picture line for line.\n\n**What each of the other three actually is.**\n\n- **A — 60-year-old man, sudden painless vision loss.** The non-arteritic AION vignette. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.193's table gives it \"Acute uniocular **painless** visual loss\" in the \"Majority > 50 years old\" row. *Painless* is what excludes optic neuritis, and the age is a generation out.\n- **C — 70-year-old diabetic with an altitudinal field defect.** The same disease again, this time named by its field. p.192: \"Field loss of either the upper or the lower half of the field of vision in the affected eye (**altitudinal field loss**)\", and p.191 adds diabetes to the atherosclerotic risk factors. An altitudinal defect respects the horizontal meridian; optic neuritis gives a **central** scotoma (p.191, `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 4).\n- **D — 35-year-old man with chronic glaucoma.** A different mechanism entirely: a slow, painless, **peripheral** loss from cupping of the disc. `L21` slide 10 shows the disc — a deeply excavated white cup with a sharp margin and vessels bayoneting over the rim — and notes that it is the **cup**, not the margin, that is abnormal. Its defect is the arcuate nerve-fibre-bundle scotoma (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.187). Nothing about it is acute.\n\n**The two elements the option list is built on** are the same two the book leads with — **who** (young woman) and **what hurts** (pain on eye movement). Note that the age and sex are a prior, not a diagnosis: a man of sixty can have optic neuritis, and a woman of twenty-five can have almost anything. What makes B decisive is that it carries the pain as well.\n\n**Why the retrobulbar form specifically.** Because the vignette gives no fundus finding. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 classifies optic neuritis as papillitis, **retrobulbar neuritis [normal optic disc]**, and neuroretinitis; a stem that names symptoms without a swollen disc is describing the form in which \"the optic disc may appear completely normal\" — the point `opqb-t14-643` keys directly.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retrobulbar optic neuritis is a disease of women aged roughly 15 to 45 presenting with subacute visual loss and periocular pain; painless loss in an older vasculopath is AION instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q641), key p.102' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-642', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'Which of the following is a **hallmark sign of retrobulbar optic neuritis**?',
    options:['Optic disc pallor on fundus exam','Afferent pupillary defect','Dilated pupil unreactive to light','Cupped optic disc'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The disease is defined by having nothing to show on the fundus, so its hallmark has to be a sign the fundus does not carry — and that is the pupil.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 sets out the signs of retrobulbar neuritis in five lines, and the first two are the whole of the answer:\n\n> 1. The optic disc **may appear completely normal**\n> 2. **Afferent pupillary defect**\n> 3. Decreased visual acuity\n> 4. Impaired colour vision\n> 5. Visual field defects.\n\nAn afferent pupillary defect means light entering the affected eye is conducted worse than light entering its fellow, so on the swinging-flashlight test both pupils **dilate** as the torch crosses to the bad eye. `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 6 photographs it in four frames and captions it *Marcus Gunn Pupil*; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.187's Tutorial figure draws it as three rows of eye pairs labelled *Constricts* and *Dilates*. `L21) Optic Nerve` slide 3 marks RAPD with a **red star**, one of only two of its five signs so flagged.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each for a different reason.**\n\n- **A — optic disc pallor.** Wrong **timing**, not wrong disease. Pallor is the *end state* — optic atrophy, which `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 files under primary for demyelinating disease. In the acute attack the disc is normal, which is the diagnostic paradox the whole entity rests on. Calling pallor the hallmark inverts the sequence.\n- **C — dilated pupil unreactive to light.** An **efferent** lesion — the third nerve, not the second. p.183's Tutorial table gives complete third nerve palsy \"Dilated non-reactive pupil and accommodation is lost (Anisocoria more in light)\". In retrobulbar neuritis the affected pupil is of **normal size** and constricts perfectly well to light shone in the *other* eye, because its own third nerve is untouched. This option is the one to read carefully: it names the wrong limb of the reflex.\n- **D — cupped optic disc.** Chronic glaucoma. `L21` slides 10 to 13 photograph it — a deeply excavated white cup, a thinned rim, vessels bayoneting over the cup wall, and a **sharp** disc margin.\n\n**The bedside formulation.** In retrobulbar neuritis *the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing* — a severely reduced acuity with a normal-looking fundus. When the fundus offers no evidence, the pupil is the objective test that proves the nerve is at fault. That aphorism is **not taken from the course material** and is offered as an aide-memoire only.\n\n**Cf. `opqb-t14-643`**, which asks the same teaching point from the disc's side, and `opqb-t14-583`, which asks it from the light reflex's.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The hallmark of retrobulbar optic neuritis is an afferent pupillary defect — an objective sign of conduction failure in an eye whose disc looks entirely normal.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q642), key p.102' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-643', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'In **retrobulbar neuritis**, the **optic disc**:',
    options:['Always appears pale','May appear completely normal','Shows cupping similar to glaucoma','Shows hemorrhages'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Retrobulbar means behind the globe. The inflammation is at a part of the nerve the ophthalmoscope cannot reach, so the visible end of the nerve looks untouched.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.190 states it twice on one page. In the classification box: \"2. **Retrobulbar neuritis [normal optic disc]**\" — the bracket is printed as part of the classification, not as a comment. And as the first of the five signs: \"1. **The optic disc may appear completely normal**\". The option is that sentence.\n\n**The contrast that gives the classification its shape.** Optic neuritis is one disease at three sites, and the site decides what the disc does:\n\n| Form | Where the inflammation sits | What the disc shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Papillitis** | at the nerve **head** | swollen and hyperaemic — p.189, \"Inflammatory edema of the disc\" |\n| **Retrobulbar neuritis** | **behind** the globe | **normal** |\n| Neuroretinitis (Tutorial) | disc and macula | disc swelling with a macular star |\n\n`L21) Optic Nerve` slide 7 photographs the papillitis end of that spectrum — a hyperaemic disc with blurred margins, and the whole image veiled and hazy, which slide 8's table explains as vitreous inflammatory cells. There is no retrobulbar photograph in the deck, and there cannot be one: a normal fundus is what it looks like.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **A — always appears pale.** Pallor comes **later**, as optic atrophy, if the attacks recur. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 files \"Demyelinating disease (MS): recurrent attacks\" under **primary** optic atrophy, whose disc `L21` slide 14 shows as chalky white with clean sharp margins. \"Always\" is what makes the option wrong even as a late statement.\n- **C — cupping similar to glaucoma.** Cupping is a slow excavation from chronic pressure, `L21` slides 10–13. Inflammation does not excavate a disc.\n- **D — haemorrhages.** Peripapillary flame haemorrhages belong to disc **swelling** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.188 item 5, \"Haemorrhage on and surrounding the nerve head\", and `L21` slide 6's papilledema photographs with dark red flame haemorrhages at the disc margin. A normal disc has none.\n\n**⚠️ Why this entry is worth reading against `opqb-t14-584`.** That question asks which type of optic atrophy follows retrobulbar neuritis and the bank keys **secondary**. The book defines secondary atrophy as \"preceded by swelling of the optic nerve head\" (p.194) — and this entry's key is that the disc in retrobulbar neuritis is normal, i.e. never swelled. The two keys pull in opposite directions on the bank's own pages. The divergence is recorded there in full and is not disputed here; both are keyed as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In retrobulbar neuritis the inflammation lies behind the globe, so the optic disc may look completely normal — pallor, cupping and haemorrhages all belong to other conditions or to a later stage.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q643), key p.102' },

  { id:'opqb-t14-644', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'Which of the following statements about **nystagmus** is **TRUE**?',
    options:['Congenital nystagmus is usually associated with oscillopsia','End-gaze nystagmus indicates central nervous system disease','Drug-induced nystagmus may appear in all gaze positions','Congenital nystagmus is typically acquired after the age of 5'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Every option is a row of one table, and three of them have been inverted.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.186 tabulates the three benign forms of nystagmus, introducing them with the sentence that answers option B: \"The three most common forms of nystagmus are **benign and do not indicate central nervous system dystunction** [sic]. These are:\"\n\n| End-gaze nystagmus | Drug-induced | Congenital nystagmus |\n|---|---|---|\n| occurs when the patient is attempting to maintain the eyes in extremes of lateral gaze | ▪ Antiepileptics, barbiturates, and other sedatives. ▪ **In this form, nystagmus may be present in all positions of gaze.** | ▪ pendular or jerk beating, and may be found **at birth or in the first 6 months of life** ▪ patients generally have subnormal vision and **do not complain of the environment shaking or moving (oscillopsia)** |\n\n**Option C is the drug-induced column's second bullet, unaltered — hence the key.** And it is a genuinely useful fact rather than trivia, because it inverts the usual rule: the book's investigation note on the same page says \"**Pathologic** forms of nystagmus are usually seen in the primary position of gaze\", so a nystagmus present in all positions would ordinarily prompt a neurological work-up. The drug history is what stops it.\n\n**Why the other three are false, each by contradicting its own row.**\n\n- **A — congenital nystagmus is usually associated with oscillopsia.** The table says the exact opposite: patients with congenital forms \"**do not** complain of the environment shaking or moving (oscillopsia)\". The brain has never known a still world and has adapted. The book puts oscillopsia on the other side: \"**Acquired** nystagmus typically causes oscillopsia and/or vertigo and may indicate vestibular, cerebellar, or brainstem dysfunction.\" So oscillopsia is the symptom that separates acquired from congenital, and this option assigns it backwards.\n- **B — end-gaze nystagmus indicates CNS disease.** Contradicts the table's own lead sentence: all three tabulated forms are **benign** and \"do not indicate central nervous system dysfunction\". End-gaze nystagmus is a physiological finding at the extremes of lateral gaze in normal people.\n- **D — congenital nystagmus is typically acquired after the age of 5.** Self-contradictory, and contradicted by the table: congenital nystagmus is \"found at birth or in the **first 6 months** of life\".\n\n**What the lecture contributes.** `L30) Squint II` slide 22 is the module's entire lecture content on nystagmus and it is four bullets: \"End Gaze · Congenital · **Drug induced** · Aquired [sic]: Vestibular, Cerebellar, Brain stem\". Its own not-covered register, item 22, records what that leaves out: \"No definition, no jerk-versus-pendular waveform, no direction convention, no null point, no head posture for nystagmus, **no drug named under 'Drug induced'**, and no treatment. No nystagmus is photographed anywhere in the deck.\" The drug names in option C's answer — antiepileptics, barbiturates and other sedatives — come from the book alone.\n\n**The clinical rule the table is really teaching:** ask when it started and whether the world moves. Present from infancy with no oscillopsia is benign; acquired with oscillopsia is a reason for a full neurological evaluation including MRI, which `ophthalmology.pdf` p.186 indicates \"for **all** patients with acquired nystagmus\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Drug-induced nystagmus from antiepileptics, barbiturates and sedatives may appear in all positions of gaze; congenital nystagmus starts by six months and causes no oscillopsia, and end-gaze nystagmus is benign.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.99 (Q644), key p.102' }
